nt through database lock: an interactive clinical trial conducted using the Internet. Drug Inf J. 2004;38(3):239–251. [ Google Scholar ] 70. Jacobs BP, Bent S, Tice JA, Blackwell T, Cummings SR. An internet-based randomized, placebo-controlled trial of kava and valerian for anxiety and insomnia. Medicine (Baltimore) 2005;84(4):197–207. doi: 10.1097/01.md.0000172299.72364.95. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 71. Wicks P, Vaughan TE, Massagli MP, Heywood J. Accelerated clinical discovery using self-reported patient data collected online and a patient-matching algorithm. Nat Biotechnol. 2011;29(5):411–414. doi: 10.1038/nbt.1837. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 72. Orri M, Lipset CH, Jacobs BP, Costello AJ, Cummings SR. Web-based trial to evaluate the efficacy and safety of tolterodine ER 4 mg in participants with overactive bladder: REMOTE trial. Contemp Clin Trials. 2014;38(2):190–197. doi: 10.1016/j.cct.2014.04.009. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 73. McAlindon T, Formica M, Kabbara K, LaValley M, Lehmer M. Conducting clinical trials over the internet: feasibility study. BMJ. 2003;327(7413):484–487. doi: 10.1136/bmj.327.7413.484. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 74. Christensen CM. The Innovator''s Dilemma: When New Technologies Cause Great Firms to Fail. Harvard Business School Press; Boston, MA: 1997. [ Google Scholar ] Associated Data This section collects any data citations, data availability statements, or supplementary materials included in this article. Supplementary Materials SupplementalMaterials NIHMS714761-supplement-SupplementalMaterials.pdf (41.4KB, pdf)',1025,'2025-10-08 11:38:18','2025-10-08 11:38:18');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(685,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7415579','Measuring Health-Related Quality of Life in Pediatric Neurology.','Lemmon ME, Huffstetler HE, Reeve BB.','2020','acquiescence discloses completes proxys neurodisability kidscreen chq kidscreen disabkids moods chq chq chq kidscreen disabkids kidscreen disabkids kidscreen kidscreen disabkids intellection outdoors chq kidscreen disabkids smiley neuroqol selfreport neuroqol healthmeasures purports mel k12ns098482 derfner wiklund shumaker aaronson saigal stoskopf pinelli devlieger pearlman uhlmann uhlmann pearlman leuthner hack kinsman donohue perinatology eiser stucky thissen raat mohangoo grootenhuis ravens sieberer herdman kidscreen reeve mcfatrich kober kober cummins comqol deakin mckinsey retard chadsey retard schalock schalock addington brit cummins thornes eiser eiser varni kober slevin drinkwater lasalvia ruggeri santolini saigal stoskopf feeny antaki rapley reese rajmil aguila upton lawford eiser bowker calarco mulberg paty coas fayed schiariti bostan strohm stam grootenhuis mcfatrich gadgil raskin gumm neurodisability weitkamp romer grefe landolt mohler viecili intellect ronen saigal neurodisabilities solans pane baca vickrey vassar beckung koning marcelli rentz matza secnik swensen revicki chq ganesan towell kirkham baars atherton koopman bullinger disabkids nowinski victorson nutakki varni swigonski varni burwinkle berrin mara meeske burwinkle varni iannaccone hynan neuromuscul toomey laverdiere dbpnet kratz slavin mulcahey tulsky coster slavin mulcahey slavin brit bertisch rivara kisala reddihough reeve wyrwich isoqol mokkink terwee coombes sangha murtagh fem terwee mokkink knol terwee mokkink knol prinsen mokkink bouter mokkink terwee terwee prinsen chiarotto','Abstract Health-related quality of life (HRQOL) is central to how clinicians and parents make choices about medical care for pediatric neurology patients. To provide parents with the information they need to make these decisions and plan for the future, it is necessary for parents and clinicians to understand how HRQOL is defined and measured in the setting of pediatric neurodevelopmental impairment. We review challenges that exist in measuring HRQOL in pediatric neurology, examine existing measures, and outline key principles to guide selection and interpretation of HRQOL measures in children with neurologic conditions. Keywords: health-related quality of life, neurology, pediatrics Introduction Improving the quality of life of pediatric neurology patients is a priority for patients, parents, clinicians, and researchers. Quality of life is central to how clinicians and parents make choices about medical care and plan for the future, and helps researchers measure the impact of interventions. To provide parents of pediatric neurology patients with the information they need to make these decisions, clinicians and researchers must understand how health-related quality of life (HRQOL) is defined and measured in the setting of pediatric neurodevelopmental impairment. HRQOL considers the impact of a disease or condition and its treatments on the lives of individuals. 1 HRQOL is multi-dimensional and incorporates physical, mental, and social aspects of health and well-being. Existing data suggest that children with neurodevelopmental disability, their families, and clinicians may define HRQOL differently. 2 These findings complement the well-known “disability paradox,” which describes how individuals living with disability rate their quality of life to be equivalent to or higher than those without disability. 3 Despite these differences, clinician interpretations of HRQOL likely influence medical decision-making, such as a clinician’s recommendation to resuscitate a patient. 4 , 5 For pediatric patients and parents, the child’s projected HRQOL is often central to discussions about the benefits and burdens of life-sustaining treatment, 6 , 7 or the pursuit of medical interventions. 8 Given these high-stakes, it is critical that clinicians, researchers, and other stakeholders have a shared understanding of how to define and measure HRQOL. Here, we review challenges that exist in measuring HRQOL in pediatric neurology, examine existing tools, and outline key principles to guide selection and interpretation of HRQOL measures in children with neurologic conditions. Challenges in HRQOL measurement Young children Historically, children have been viewed as unable to provide accurate assessments of their own HRQOL. 9 Over the past twenty years, studies have increasingly involved children more directly in the assessment of their own health. 10 – 13 Yet, there are logistical challenges when measuring HRQOL in young children. Written measures are rarely appropriate until the child is at least eight years of age. Lengthy measures may be difficult for young children to complete. Children’s cognitive, emotional, and physical development often changes rapidly over time, presenting both logistical and conceptual challenges to measuring HRQOL. In the context of disease or disability, a child’s development may be delayed or follow an atypical trajectory. Young children may also fundamentally understand or experience the impact of illness differently than adolescents or adults; 9 the salience of different aspects of an individual’s life can vary according to their state of development and personal frame of reference. 14 For example, a young child may define physical functioning in terms of their ability to play individually or with friends; whereas an adolescent or adult may define physical functioning in terms of their ability to go to school, to work, or to move about in their environment. Cognitive Impairment Many children with neurologic conditions experience cognitive impairment. Similar to young children, assessing HRQOL in children with cognitive disabilities can present logistical challenges. For example, children with severe cognitive impairment may lack formal communication skills which can limit the ability of families, clinicians, and other individuals to establish meaningful communication and gather primary information about HRQOL. 15 Tailored approaches have been developed to assess HRQOL in individuals with less severe cognitive disability; 16 – 19 however, factors such as acquiescence can negatively affect both the validity and reliability of these assessments. 20 There also exists a tension between the desire for self-reported outcomes and the suitability and limitations of current HRQOL measures; it may be difficult for some children with cognitive impairment to conceptualize and assess their own HRQOL. 14 When children with cognitive impairment cannot self-report HRQOL, proxy- and observer-reported measures can be used as a complement to or substitute for self-reported outcomes. Parent proxy or observer-reported measurement The use of proxy- and observer-reported outcomes can circumvent some of the challenges associated with assessing HRQOL in young patients and children who cannot self-report. In observer-reported outcomes, an individual discloses observations about a patient. In proxy-reported outcomes, an individual completes the measure as if they are the patient themselves. Both proxy and observer reports have limitations. Proxy HRQOL assessments, often completed by a parent, may be influenced by the proxy’s own feelings about and experiences of caring for the patient, 21 , 22 and sometimes, stakeholders may not agree on what constitutes an acceptable long-term outcome. A number of studies show that there can be significant discordance between self- and proxy-reports of HRQOL, and that proxys can both over- and underestimate HRQOL. 23 – 25 Additional work has shown that clinician-observer assessments of HRQOL also differ from HRQOL assessments of patients and proxies. 26 – 28 Due to the variation in the accuracy of proxy- and observer-reported data, it is necessary that the individuals conducting assessments or providing proxy information possess a large degree of mutual understanding with the patient. 29 , 30 The accuracy of proxy and observer assessment may depend on HRQOL domain; literature suggests that proxies and observers assess physical domains of HRQOL more accurately than emotional domains. 23 , 24 Parent-child agreement may further depend on age; one study suggests that parent-child discordance increases over time. 31 Importantly, these discrepancies may simply reflect differences in belief about the child’s well-being; proxy- and child-reports contribute complementary information to overall HRQOL assessments. 32 For these reasons and others, FDA guidance suggests that observer-reported outcomes should be limited to observable events and behaviors for children unable to self-report. 33 Existing Tools There are two broad types of HRQOL measures: generic and condition-specific. Generic measures assess HRQOL domains that are relevant across a wide variety of populations, and are often used to compare HRQOL scores across populations to provide contrasting information on condition- and treatment-related burden. Condition-specific measures assess HRQOL domains of specific relevance to a particular condition or patient population. HRQOL measures have been used in a variety of pediatric disciplines, including pediatric oncology and pediatric palliative care. Despite their wide use, there is little consensus on an “ideal” measure in these disciplines, with persistent debates on which aspects of HRQOL are most important to capture and limited research on the reliability and validity of existing tools. For example, systematic reviews in pediatric oncology suggest that few HRQOL measures have been rigorously tested for a range of psychometric properties. 34 – 36 A recent review in pediatric neurosurgical populations highlighted the variability in application of HRQOL metrics, and the relatively limited study of psychometric properties within pediatric neurosurgical populations. 37 A 2016 systematic review of patient-reported outcome measures in neurodisability found that no existing instrument has been evaluated across the full range of relevant measurement properties. 38 For children with neurodevelopmental impairment, many studies have relied on generic instruments, such as the KIDSCREEN, Child Health Questionnaire, or PROMIS Pediatric, to measure HRQOL, allowing for direct comparisons across diverse clinical populations. 39 – 43 Others have developed and applied condition-specific measures, including over a dozen measures applicable to children with neurologic conditions, including pediatric epilepsy, spina bifida, cerebral palsy, and hydrocephalus. 36 , 44 , 45 Here, we highlight five widely used measures studied in patients with neurodevelopmental impairment, each measuring a different set of HRQOL domains ( Table 1 ). Table 1. Comparison of domains in common measures of HRQOL CHQ KIDSCREEN DISABKIDS (Generic Measure) Neuro-QOL PedsQL (Generic Measure) PROMIS Pediatric Physical ● Physical functioning ● Role functioning: physical ● Bodily pain ● Physical well-being ● Physical health - Limitation - Treatment ● Physical health - Fatigue - Pain - Lower extremity mobility - Upper extremity fine motor ADL ● Physical functioning ● Physical health - Asthma impact - Pain-behavior - Pain-interference - Pain quality - Physical activity - Physical function: mobility - Physical function: upper extremity - Physical stress experience - Sleep-related disturbance - Sleep-related impairment - Strength impact Emotional ● Role functioning: emotional ● Self-esteem ● Mental health Psychological well-being ● Moods and emotions ● Self-perception ● Mental health - Independence - Emotion ● Mental health - Cognitive function - Anger - Anxiety - Depression ● Emotional functioning ● Mental health - Cognitive function - Anger - Anxiety - Depression - Life satisfaction - Meaning and purpose - Positive affect - Psychological stress experiences Social ● Family activities ● Family cohesion ● Social support and peers ● Social acceptance and bullying ● Parent relation and home life ● Social health - Social inclusion - Social exclusion ● Social health - Social relations: peers - Social relations: adults - Stigma - Depression ● Social functioning ● Social health - Peer relationships - Family relationships Other ● General health perceptions ● Parent impact: emotion ● Parent impact: time ● Role functioning: behavior ● General behavior ● Change in health ● Autonomy ● Financial resources ● School environment ● School functioning Open in a new tab Child Health Questionnaire (CHQ) The Child Health Questionnaire™ (CHQ) is a family of generic measures that assess HRQOL in children and adolescents. These measures come in both long and short forms that all assess thirteen physical and psychosocial HRQOL domains. Self-report measures are available for children between ten and eighteen years old, and proxy-report measures are available for children between five to eighteen years of age. These measures require registration and potential fees for use, can be administered on paper or by computer, and can be scored using statistical software. The CHQ has been used to assess HRQOL in multiple pediatric populations with neurodevelopmental disabilities, including children with epilepsy, 46 cerebral palsy, 47 ADHD, 48 and stroke. 49 KIDSCREEN and DISABKIDS The KIDSCREEN project developed generic measures to assess HRQOL in children and adolescents. The measures address up to ten domains related to the physical, mental, and social aspects of HRQOL. These measures can be administered in either short form (addressing 5 HRQOL domains), long form (addressing 10 HRQOL domains), or index version, which provides a global score. Self-report and proxy-report measures are available for children and youth between the ages of eight and eighteen years old. 12 The DISABKIDS project, building upon the KIDSCREEN project, contains disease-specific measures to assess pediatric HRQOL as specifically influenced by chronic health conditions. Condition-specific modules, including modules for children with cerebral palsy and epilepsy, were designed to complement the generic module by addressing condition-specific concerns, such as anxiety about having seizures. 50 Both the KIDSCREEN and DISABKIDS measures require registration and potential fees for use, can be administered via multiple modalities, and can be scored by hand or using statistical software. Neuro-QOL As part of an effort initiated by the National Institute of Neurological Disorders and Stroke, a group of researchers developed a disease-specific HRQOL measure for adults and children with neurologic conditions (Neuro-QOL). HRQOL domains targeting children with neurological conditions—such as fatigue, pain, cognition, and stigma—were evaluated in general pediatric populations and children with epilepsy or muscular dystrophy. 51 The Neuro-QOL includes self-report measures for children and youth between ten to seventeen years old; when needed, these measures can be completed by a proxy. These measures are free and publically available, can be administered via multiple modalities, and can be scored by hand or using a free scoring service. PedsQL The PedsQL generic measure assesses HRQOL across the four domains of physical, emotional, social, and school functioning, and has been used to measure HRQOL in the general pediatric population as well as children with medical conditions. Self-report measures are available for children between five and eighteen years old, and proxy-report measures are available for children between two and eighteen years old. The PedsQL also offers condition-specific modules, which are intended to be used in adjunct with the generic measure to assess overall HRQOL. These condition-specific modules can be used to assess various factors that influence HRQOL in pediatric populations with particular neurodevelopmental disabilities, including children with neurofibromatosis, cerebral palsy, epilepsy, brain tumors, and neuromuscular disabilities. 52 – 56 These measures require potential fees for use, can be administered on paper or in person, and can be scored by hand or using statistical software. PROMIS Pediatric Measures The NIH’s Patient-Reported Outcomes Measurement Information System ® (PROMIS ® ) provides a broad set of pediatric self-report and proxy measures to assess HRQOL for the general population and for children who have chronic conditions. Addressing more than twenty-two domains across physical, mental, and social aspects of HRQOL, these measures can be administered in short form for a single domain (e.g., mobility, anxiety) or in “profiles” that include multiple HRQOL domains across physical, mental, and social health. The PROMIS has 3 profile instruments, including the PROMIS-25 (4 items per domain), PROMIS-37 (6 items per domain), and PROMIS-49 (8 items per domain). Self-report measures are available for children between eight and seventeen years old, and proxy-report measures are available for children between five and seventeen years old. These measures are free and publically available, can be administered via multiple modalities, and can be scored by hand or using a free scoring service. A number of studies have evaluated the use of the PROMIS pediatric measures in populations with neurodevelopmental disabilities, including children with autism, 57 cerebral palsy, 58 – 60 and traumatic brain injury. 61 Finding the right measure: Challenges and Opportunities There is increasing interest in understanding the impact of pediatric neurologic conditions on patient and family quality of life. This interest provides an opportunity to ensure measures are high quality and important to stakeholders. Each measure offers unique strengths and weaknesses for a given research question. For researchers and clinicians who wish to find or develop the appropriate measure for their population, there are several key questions to consider ( Figure 1 , Table 1 ). Figure 1. Open in a new tab Considerations and guiding questions for choosing HRQOL instruments First, identify which HRQOL domains are important to assess and select a measure that captures the relevant domains of interest. To ensure that measures include domains most helpful to families, stakeholders must be involved in the development and selection of tools. Formative work by Davis et al. interviewed parents of children with intellection disability and cerebral palsy to identify which domains of quality of life are most relevant to these children and the families who care from them. 62 Several of these domains are distinct from those included in commonly used generic measures, including body comfort, predictability and routine, independence and autonomy, variety of activity, nature and outdoors, and access to services. These domains present an opportunity to develop novel, parent-informed measures of HRQOL for children with neurodevelopmental impairment. 62 There are many conditions for which this formative work has not yet been completed; understanding which domains are most appropriate for patients and those caring for critically ill children and infants with neurological conditions should be a priority for the field. The FDA has provided clear guidance on the best practices in development of patient-reported outcomes, including measures of HRQOL; this process is labor-intensive, and requires inter-professional teams with expertise in measure development, mixed methods analysis, and stakeholder engagement (cite). Second, consider the unique features of the patient population. If the patient population is young or impacted by cognitive impairment, choose measures with a strong observer- and/or proxy-report component. If the child can self-report, use patient-reported outcome measures with developmentally appropriate questions. Some studies may incorporate more than one source for HRQOL (e.g., patient-report and parent-report) to capture complementary viewpoints on the child’s condition. While many research questions can be answered with a single measure of HRQOL, there may be situations in which administering more than one measure has advantages. For example, an investigator interested in pediatric epilepsy may hope to understand how HRQOL in pediatric epilepsy differs from HRQOL in other chronic pediatric health conditions; thus, a generic measure (for example, the PROMIS or PedsQL) will allow for cross-condition comparisons, while an epilepsy-specific measure will include additional HRQOL domains that are only relevant to epilepsy. Administrative burden, cost, and survey length may dictate what is feasible for a given investigator. Some instruments have fees; fees are especially common for studies funded by industry. Others require training to administer. Survey length varies dramatically between measures. Many measures have both short and long forms; participant burden is the primary reason to use a short form measure. Long form measures are likely to have stronger reliability and content validity, and are more likely to demonstrate change over time. Long form measures are recommended when the HRQOL construct assessed by the measure is a study’s primary or secondary outcome. Resources (e.g., survey length and cost) are key factors that determine measure choice for many investigators. For a junior investigator with minimal resources looking to study HRQOL within a patient cohort at their local institution, a free, easy to administer, and short measure may be ideal. For investigators planning a multi-site study of HRQOL among adolescents with epilepsy, a suite of measures, including a generic measure and an instrument specific to pediatric epilepsy populations may be more appropriate ( Table 2 ). Table 2. Characteristics of HRQOL measures. Measure Respondent Length Registration & Cost Administration & Scoring # Domains # Items CHQ Self-report (ages 10–18) Proxy (for children 5–18) 14 47 (self-report short form) 87 (self-report long form) 28 (proxy short form) 50 (proxy long form) ● Must register for use ● Requires request for pricing ● Can be administered on paper or by computer ● No special training required to administer ● Can be scored using statistical software KIDSCREEN Self-report (ages 8–18) Proxy (for children 8–18) Single scale 5 10 10 (short form) 27 (short form) 57 (long form) ● Must register for use. ● Free to administer for non-commercial studies. Fees apply for commercial studies. ● Can be administered on paper, by telephone, in-person interview, or by computer ● No special training required to administer ● Can be scored by hand or using statistical software DISABKIDS (Generic Measures) Self-report (ages 4–18) Proxy (for children 4–18) 6 6 (“Smiley” measure) 12 (short form) 37 (long form) ● Must register for use ● Manual must be purchased for a fee ● Free to administer for government-funded academic research and unfunded research. Fees apply for other study types. ● Can be administered on paper, by telephone, in-person interview, by computer ● No specialized training required to administer ● Can be scored by hand or using statistical software NeuroQOL Self-report (ages 10–17) * 11 Each domain is typically assessed by 4–12 items, but varies based on which form is used ● Free and publically available ● Can be administered on paper (short forms only), by computer, or within an app ● No specialized training required to administer or score ** PedsQL (Generic Measure) Self-report (ages 5–18) Proxy (for children 2–18) 4 23 ● Free for non-funded academic research. Fees apply for other study types. ● Can be administered on paper or through in-person interview ● Intended to be administered by individuals trained in the administration of standardized questionnaires ● Can be scored by hand or using statistical software PROMIS Pediatric Self-report (ages 8–17) Proxy (for children 5–17) 25 (selfreport) 22 (proxy) Each domain is typically assessed by 4–12 items, but varies based on which form is used ● Free and publically available ● Can be administered on paper (short forms and profiles only), by computer, or within an app ● No specialized training required to administer or score ** Open in a new tab * Can be completed by proxy if needed ** Can be scored by using a data collection tool that includes NeuroQOL and PROMIS measures, free scoring service provided by HealthMeasures, or scored by hand Finally, assess the psychometric properties of the HRQOL measure. Key properties should include reliability, validity, responsiveness, and a scoring system that is easy to interpret and understand. 63 , 64 Reliability is defined as the extent the HRQOL measure is free from measurement error; relevant indices include internal consistency reliability, test-retest reliability, and inter-rater reliability (if using an observer-reported HRQOL measure). 63 – 66 Adequate reliability is generally defined as ≥0.70 for group-level comparisons. 63 , 67 Validity is defined as the degree to which the measure assesses the construct it purports to measure, and includes content, criterion, and construct validity. 63 – 66 Responsiveness refers to the measure’s ability to detect change over time; selecting tools with appropriate responsiveness is particularly important for longitudinal studies. The psychometric properties of each measure may vary between patient populations; a tool with strong psychometric evidence in pediatric epilepsy populations, for example, may not perform as well or have the same level of data for patients with muscular dystrophy. In this example, content validity—the extent to which the measure captures those HRQOL domains salient to the patient population—likely differs between these populations. As a result, an investigator should begin by reviewing the literature for the psychometric data of a given measure in their condition of interest, or in closely related conditions. Consensus-based standards can be an added resource for investigators interested in learning more about the selection of health measurements. 63 , 64 , 67 – 69 The growing interest in measuring and improving HRQOL in pediatric neurology patients is encouraging. Here, we outline unique features of pediatric neurology conditions that warrant a targeted approach to measurement development and selection. Acknowledgments Funding : MEL is supported by the National Institute of Neurological Disorders and Stroke of the National Institutes of Health under award number K12NS098482 and the Derfner Foundation. Footnotes Declaration of conflicting interests : The authors have no conflicts of interest to disclose. REFERENCES 1. Cella DF, Wiklund I, Shumaker SA, Aaronson NK. Integrating health-related quality of life into cross-national clinical trials. Qual Life Res. 1993;2(6):433–440. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Saigal S, Stoskopf B, Pinelli J, et al. Self-perceived health-related quality of life of former extremely low birth weight infants at young adulthood. Pediatrics. 2006;118(3):1140–1148. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Albrecht GL, Devlieger PJ. The disability paradox: high quality of life against all odds. Soc Sci Med. 1999;48(8):977–988. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Starr TJ, Pearlman RA, Uhlmann RF. Quality of life and resuscitation decisions in elderly patients. J Gen Intern Med. 1986;1(6):373–379. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Uhlmann RF, Pearlman RA. Perceived quality of life and preferences for life-sustaining treatment in older adults. Arch Intern Med. 1991;151(3):495–497. [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Leuthner SR. Decisions regarding resuscitation of the extremely premature infant and models of best interest. J Perinatol. 2001;21(3):193–198. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 7. Hack M Consideration of the use of health status, functional outcome, and quality-of-life to monitor neonatal intensive care practice. Pediatrics. 1999;103(1 