INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(686,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7844157','Rapid Implementation of Virtual Health in a Pediatric Neurology Practice During COVID-19.','Lee J, Schrader D, Boelman C, Huh L, Connolly MB.','2020','rapide virtuelles neuropediatrie passee rapidement virtuelles totalite externes donnaient virtuellement seulement tenaient personne pourtant enregistre precedents neurologues degre physique revele fiable rarement necessaire recourir complementaires personne rapide prestation prolongee visites virtuelles neuropediatrie permet fournir comparables donnes personne lorsque distanciation physique imposent rametta bcch cancelations bcch skype cancelations bcch cancelling cancelation rametta dysmorphology neurophysiologic fontanelles joelle pellegrin mandeep tamber s0317167120002413sup001 larner hatcher rasmusson hartshorn qubty patniyot e1702 velasquez carballo utidjian gospe schwamm erskine licurse e282 demasi lunagariya jani e4410 glidden birbeck schwamm balcer rametta fridinger s0317167120002413sup001','ABSTRACT: During the COVID-19 pandemic, the Division of Neurology at BC Children’s Hospital rapidly transitioned to almost exclusively virtual health. In April 2020, 96% of outpatient visits were done virtually (64%) or by telephone, and only 4.2% were in-person. Total clinic visit numbers were unchanged compared to previous months. Neurologists reported high satisfaction with the virtual history and overall assessment, while the physical examination was less reliable. Additional in-person visits were rarely required. Rapid, sustained adoption of virtual health is possible in a pediatric neurology setting, providing reliable care that is comparable to in-person consultations when physical distancing is necessary. Keywords: Neurological practice, Neurology – pediatric RÉSUMÉ : Passage rapide aux consultations virtuelles dans un service de neuropédiatrie durant la pandémie de COVID-19. Durant la pandémie de COVID-19, la division du service de neurologie au BC Children’s Hospital est passée rapidement aux consultations virtuelles, et ce, en quasi-totalité. En avril 2020, 96 % des consultations externes se donnaient virtuellement (64 %) ou par téléphone, et seulement 4,2 % des autres se tenaient en personne. Pourtant, le nombre total de consultations cliniques n’a pas changé par rapport à celui enregistré les mois précédents. De plus, les neurologues ont fait état d’un degré élevé de satisfaction au regard de la prise virtuelle de l’anamnèse et de l’évaluation globale, mais l’examen physique, lui, s’est révélé moins fiable. Il a rarement été nécessaire de recourir aux consultations complémentaires en personne. Ainsi, l’adoption rapide et la prestation prolongée de visites virtuelles est possible en neuropédiatrie, ce qui permet de fournir des soins de qualité, comparables à ceux donnés durant les consultations en personne lorsque des mesures de distanciation physique s’imposent. Telehealth, the delivery of health care using electronic communication, allows patients to access health care remotely, either from a local satellite site 1 (often referred to as telemedicine), or from home using a personal electronic device (specifically referred to as virtual health). Non-inferiority of telehealth evaluations and comparable efficacy to in-person visits has been demonstrated 2 , 3 and telehealth in pediatric neurology results in high patient satisfaction, reducing time, travel, and cost. 4 , 5 The importance of history and visual inspection 1 , 4 , 6 in the clinical evaluation, especially in young children, and the concentration of pediatric neurologists in urban areas 6 – 8 make telehealth well suited for pediatric neurology, though limitations in examination have restricted its use largely to follow-up visits. 9 The COVID-19 pandemic has highlighted many advantages of virtual health, such as ability of both patient and provider to remain at home, 10 greater convenience, and reduced cost. 11 , 12 Recently, Grossman and colleagues described a successful transition to almost exclusively virtual adult neurology outpatient care during the COVID-19 pandemic. 13 Rametta and colleagues assessed the implementation of remote telehealth services in pediatric neurology in response to the COVID-19 outbreak by comparing 2093 virtual encounters with previous in-person encounters. They found high satisfaction among providers, and a low rate of in-person encounters following the virtual visit. 14 Many children in British Columbia live far from specialist care. In 2019, telemedicine accounted for 4.6% of total encounters within the Division of Neurology at BC Children’s Hospital (BCCH), the only large, publicly funded, academic hospital-based pediatric neurology practice in the province. Prior to the COVID crisis, 12 of the 14 pediatric neurologists in our practice had used telemedicine, while only 4 had done virtual health in a pilot study comparing virtual health to conventional telemedicine. We assessed the impact of large-scale transition to virtual health on overall clinic visit numbers, obtained from internal booking data. We assessed physician-reported satisfaction with virtual health by means of two surveys. We hypothesized that prior experience with telehealth would facilitate a successful and widespread transition to virtual health, minimizing cancelations and maintaining clinic visit volumes. On March 16, 2020, physical distancing restrictions were enacted at BCCH and all non-urgent outpatient in-person and telehealth visits were converted to virtual health or telephone consultation or postponed. It was agreed that all new and follow-up outpatients would be seen by virtual health, except those with a suspected acutely evolving neurological condition, medical instability, or need for in-person physical examination, who would be seen by the on-call team where possible. Virtual visits were done using Skype for Business and Zoom, which were compliant with provincial confidentiality regulations. Rapid conversion to a largely virtual health model of care required training and credentialing for the neurologists and clerical staff. By the end of April 2020, the first full month with physical distancing measures, the pediatric neurology clinic successfully implemented a large-scale transition to virtual health. By July, 13 (93%) pediatric neurologists had used virtual health. In April, virtual visits accounted for 63.1% (365) of visits, and in-person visits made up only 4.1% (24), with the remaining 32.8% by telephone (combined virtual and telephone consultations accounted for 95.9% of all encounters). This increase was sustained, with 61.6% of all visits between April and July done virtually (Figure 1 ), while increased in-person encounters in the summer months were offset by a corresponding decrease in telephone consultations. Figure 1: Open in a new tab Physical distancing measures were implemented in mid-March 2020. In April 2020, virtual health visits represented 63.1% of all outpatient neurology visits. The number of virtual health visits remained consistent through July 2020, while a rebound in the number of in-person visits was largely offset by a decrease in telephone consultations. The median number of monthly visits between March and July 2020 (596) was comparable to the median in 2019 (576). Monthly visit numbers during the pandemic were also similar to the corresponding months in 2019 (Supplementary table). New patients were seen by virtual health and overall at a rate comparable to baseline. The types of patients seen by virtual health did not differ from baseline, with no subspecialty neurology clinics reporting increased cancelations or delays due to the transition to virtual health. In some instances, such as neuromuscular clinics, challenges were noted related to the need for a complete physical examination, particularly among new patients, though visit numbers did not decrease. Virtual health utilization was greater in neurology compared to combined medical subspecialties at BCCH, where combined virtual health and phone encounters accounted for only 64% of all visits in March and April. Medical specialities also saw a 24% decrease in overall patient visits compared to the same period in 2019. Two surveys were distributed to the 11 neurologists who had seen patients by virtual health. These were done as part of a quality improvement initiative and ethical approval was not required. A brief post-encounter survey assessed whether the virtual encounter was an adequate substitute for an in-person visit. Sixty-nine responses were received between March 17 and April 20, each corresponding to a single virtual health visit. In 57 (83.8%) encounters, the neurologist either strongly agreed or agreed with the statement “Virtual Health was an adequate substitute for an in-person encounter” while 3 (4.4%) disagreed. Sixty-four (92.75%) patients seen would have the same follow-up plan as if they had been seen in person, while five required an additional in-person visit to obtain additional information (typically from the physical examination). The low response rate (69 of 400 visits) may make this survey prone to selection bias. The second survey assessed patterns of use, attitudes about efficacy and safety, and satisfaction. All 11 pediatric neurologists who did virtual consultations responded to the survey. Eight (73%) neurologists did 75% or more of their total clinic encounters following the COVID-19 outbreak using virtual health, three exclusively. All respondents strongly agreed or agreed with the statement “Virtual Health allowed me to avoid postponing or cancelling clinic visits due to the COVID-19 outbreak.” Nine (82%) strongly agreed or agreed with the statement “I was satisfied with my ability to obtain an accurate history,” though only three (27%) respondents agreed “I was satisfied with my ability to obtain physical examination findings,” five (45%) disagreed, and three were neutral. Nine (82%) respondents strongly agreed or agreed that they were satisfied with their ability to form an assessment and management plan. Follow-up plans from virtual visits were similar to in-person visits; seven (64%) respondents needed to schedule an additional in-person visit following a virtual visit less than 25% of the time. All 11 respondents agreed (9 (82%) strongly agreeing) “I would recommend the use of virtual health to my colleagues.” Ten (91%) either strongly or agreed “I plan to continue using Virtual Health to assess patients even after physical distancing restrictions are relaxed.” Perceived patient comfort (10/11 responses) and avoiding appointment cancelation (11/11 responses) were the most frequently cited advantages of virtual health. Virtual health was felt to be superior to telephone consultations for building rapport (11/11 responses), which was not surprising as most pediatric telephone consults exclude the patient. Cost- and time-savings to families were also noted as important advantages. The inability to do physical examinations in person (8/11) and technical problems (10/11) were frequently encountered limitations. The clinician survey assessed neurologists’ overall impression of virtual health at a single point following multiple encounters, which may have made this survey subject to recall bias. Virtual health was considered an adequate substitute for in-person visits, and the good overall satisfaction with virtual health supports its suitability in pediatric neurology for new and follow-up patients. Our findings are similar to those from a recent publication by Rametta and colleagues, 14 who compared pediatric neurology telehealth visits to in-person encounters during the COVID-19 pandemic and found high clinician satisfaction and relatively little need for repeat visits. The similar experiences between our institution, a single, urban, Canadian center serving an entire province, and a large US network with multiple satellite locations, suggest that virtual pediatric neurology care can be successfully employed across different health care systems. Pediatric neurology-specific clinical factors likely facilitated the transition to virtual health. Many aspects of the neurological examination can be done virtually, including speech and cognition; observations of behavior; some of the cranial nerve exam; some aspects of motor examination, coordination, and gait; and key extra-neurologic features such as dysmorphology and cutaneous examination. Relatively little of outpatient pediatric neurology is procedural, and most tests or treatments are not performed in clinic. Neurophysiologic, radiologic, and genetic investigations can easily be reviewed remotely. Limitations include the inability to assess vital signs, fundoscopy, muscle weakness, tendon reflexes, tone abnormalities, sensation, and aspects of surface anatomy such as cranial sutures and fontanelles, among others. Future work should include development of guidelines for appropriate patient selection, a standardized clinical approach to the pediatric neurological virtual examination, and evaluation of patient safety. Formal assessment of patient satisfaction would be valuable. We describe the rapid, successful, and sustained transition of a publicly funded Canadian academic pediatric neurology outpatient practice to predominantly virtual health as a result of the COVID-19 crisis, with patient volumes maintained at levels comparable to baseline despite physical distancing, and high provider satisfaction. Successful integration of virtual health to complement in-person consultation has the potential to transform the practice of pediatric neurology and improve access to care. Acknowledgments The authors thank the pediatric neurologists at BC Children’s Hospital for their support of this project. The authors thank Child Health BC, including Joelle Pellegrin, the clerical staff in the Division of Neurology, and leadership at BC Children’s Hospital, for their support using virtual health. The authors are also grateful to Dr. Mandeep Tamber, who provided mentorship, and to the Hudson Family for supporting the neurology telehealth initiative. Disclosure Statement The authors have no conflicts of interests to declare. Statement of Authorship All authors (JL, DS, CB, LH, and MC) made substantial contributions to the conception and/or design of this article. JL drafted the manuscript, which all authors revised. All authors gave final approval of the version and agreed to be accountable for all aspects of the work Supplementary material For supplementary material accompanying this paper visit https://doi.org/10.1017/cjn.2020.241. S0317167120002413sup001.docx (16.4KB, docx) click here to view supplementary material References 1. Larner AJ. Teleneurology: an overview of current status. Pract Neurol. 2011;11(5):283–88. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Hatcher-Martin JM, Adams JL, Anderson ER, et al. Telemedicine in neurology: Telemedicine Work Group of the American Academy of Neurology update. Neurology. 2020;94(1):30–38. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Rasmusson KA, Hartshorn JC. A comparison of epilepsy patients in a traditional ambulatory clinic and a telemedicine clinic. Epilepsia. 2005;46(5):767–70. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Wechsler LR. Advantages and limitations of teleneurology. JAMA Neurol. 2015;72(3):349–54. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Qubty W, Patniyot I, Gelfand A. Telemedicine in a pediatric headache clinic: a prospective survey. Neurology. 2018;90(19):e1702–705. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Joshi C. Telemedicine in pediatric neurology. Pediatr Neurol. 2014;51(2):189–91. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 7. Velasquez SE, Chaves-Carballo E, Nelson EL. Pediatric teleneurology: a model of epilepsy care for rural populations. Pediatr Neurol. 2016;64:32–37. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 8. Utidjian L, Abramson E. Pediatric telehealth: opportunities and challenges. Pediatr Clin North Am. 2016;63(2):367–78. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 9. Lo MD, Gospe SM. Telemedicine and child neurology. J Child Neurol. 2019;34(1):22–26. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 10. Schwamm LH, Estrada J, Erskine A, Licurse A. Virtual care: new models of caring for our patients and workforce [published online ahead of print, 2020 May 6]. Lancet Digit Health. 2020;2(6):e282–85. doi: 10.1016/S2589-7500(20)30104-7 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 11. Patel UK, Malik P, DeMasi M, Lunagariya A, Jani VB. Multidisciplinary approach and outcomes of Tele-neurology: a review. Cureus. 2019;11(4):e4410. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 12. Dorsey ER, Glidden AM, Holloway MR, Birbeck GL, Schwamm LH. Teleneurology and mobile technologies: the future of neurological care. Nat Rev Neurol. 2018;14(5):285–97. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 13. Grossman SN, Han SC, Balcer LJ, et al. Rapid implementation of virtual neurology in response to the COVID-19 pandemic. Neurology. 2020;94(24):1077–87. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 14. Rametta SC, Fridinger SE, Gonzalez AK, et al. Analyzing 2,589 child neurology telehealth encounters necessitated by the COVID-19 pandemic [published online ahead of print, 2020 Jun 9]. Neurology. 2020;95(9):10.1212/WNL.0000000000010010. doi: 10.1212/WNL.0000000000010010 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Associated Data This section collects any data citations, data availability statements, or supplementary materials included in this article. Supplementary Materials For supplementary material accompanying this paper visit https://doi.org/10.1017/cjn.2020.241. S0317167120002413sup001.docx (16.4KB, docx) click here to view supplementary material',17,'2025-10-08 11:38:47','2025-10-08 11:38:47');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(687,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10985310','Sleeping with one cerebrum open: patient and staff perceptions of sleep quality and quantity on an inpatient neurology unit.','Kadura S, Poulakis A, Roberts DE, Arora VM, Darrow SN, Eisner L, Ibarra M, Lin J, Wang L, Pigeon WR.','2024','noises nonepileptic overstated kadura poulakis cerebrum overestimating awakenings misalignment nonepileptic doorway hallway accommodates nightshift dayshift noises phsdnq aloud transcribing epworth epworth somnolence snoring awakenings ksd ksd phrased refreshing phrased refreshed phrased soundly soundly phrased woke slept phrased ksd ksd noises phsdnq toileting phsdnq phsdnq dichotomize phsdnq ksd nonrefreshing ksd nonrefreshing shiftwork evenings nonepileptic preadmission preadmission preadmission epworth epworth nonepileptic tolerating awakenings noises noises nonrefreshing toileting refreshed soundly toileting nonrefreshing soundly woke slept nonstroke noncritical bundling woken disturb penalize phsdnq nonrestorative ringers roommates snoring ringer earplugs earplugs toileting phsdnq ksd nonrefreshing nightmares bolder televisions disoriented roommates offending awakenings ksd worklists misalignment worklists worklists noncontributory nonessential awakenings unfounded nightshift understated overstated individualizing safwan badr mesmer oskar wielgus biostatistical epworth ksd phsdnq noises wesselius alsma onderzoeks geneeskunde venkateshiah collop gamaldo chik huffman rasquinha hoesch depietro knutson spampinato cauter clocks sivathamboo farrand e194 e204 duss gambelli bernasconi nobili beniczky plomaritis theodorou lourentzos peirce erondu gozal worku awakenings redeker pisani yaggi knauert dobing frolova mcalister ringrose e0156735 qiongjing qiuli sabrina xiaojing changli indermuhle shanks dubose hadi epworth buysse ancoli edinger krystal lichstein akerstedt hume waterhouse intraindividual percept mot keklund akerstedt akerstedt hume waterhouse balachandran mokhlesi spampinato knutson erondu peirce paudel zsx037 keesenberg merten gemke rjbj nanayakkara pwb bartick altaye solet e24 stiver growdon inouye hshieh puelle dowal travison inouye dykes kadura siala haren mourad niekerk','Abstract Study Objectives: This study sought to investigate perceptions of sleep disruptions among patients and staff in the inpatient neurology setting. The objectives were to explore the differences between these groups regarding factors that impact sleep, identify the most significant sleep disruptions, and examine the barriers and opportunities suggested to improve inpatient sleep. Methods: A survey-based observational study was conducted on a 25-bed inpatient neurology unit at an academic medical center. Staff and patients completed the Potential Hospital Sleep Disruptions and Noises Questionnaire, and focus groups were held to gather qualitative data. Patient-reported sleep measures were collected for additional assessment. Responses were dichotomized for comparison. Regression models were used to assess associations between disruptors and patient-reported sleep measures. Qualitative thematic analyses were performed. Results: Forty-nine inpatient staff and 247 patients completed sleep surveys. Top primary patient diagnoses included stroke, epilepsy, autoimmune diseases, and psychogenic nonepileptic attacks. Medical interventions, environmental factors, patient-related factors, and unit workflows emerged as key themes related to sleep disruptions. Patient-reported sleep efficiency was significantly reduced when pain, anxiety, stress, temperature, and medication administration disrupted sleep. Staff perspectives highlighted medical interventions as most disruptive to sleep, while patients did not find them as disruptive as expected. Conclusions: Differing perspectives on sleep disruption exist between staff and patients in the inpatient neurology setting. Medical interventions may be overstated in staff perceptions and inpatient sleep research, as pain, anxiety, and stress had the most significant impact on patient-reported sleep efficiency. Citation: Kadura S, Poulakis A, Roberts DE, et al. Sleeping with one cerebrum open: patient and staff perceptions of sleep quality and quantity on an inpatient neurology unit. J Clin Sleep Med . 2024;20(4):619–629. Keywords: sleep, inpatient, neurology, sleep disruption, nurses, hospitalized patients, perceptions, multidisciplinary, hospitals, patient-centered BRIEF SUMMARY Current Knowledge/Study Rationale: To the authors’ knowledge, this study is the first to examine patient and staff perceptions of sleep disruptions and their impact on patient-reported sleep quality in a neurology inpatient unit through surveys, open-ended questions, and focus groups. Previous studies focused primarily on general medicine and intensive care environments and utilized surveys for data collection. Study Impact: This study demonstrates that patients and staff have significant differences in perceptions of inpatient sleep disruptions, with staff underestimating the impact of patient factors, such as pain, stress, or anxiety while overestimating the impact of medical interventions such as vital sign checks. Patient factors also had the most significant impact on decreasing patient-reported sleep efficiency compared to medical interventions and environmental factors. INTRODUCTION Sleep in the hospital is often disrupted despite its importance in healing and recovery. Compared to sleep at home, hospitalized patients have reduced total sleep time, earlier wake-up times, and increased nocturnal awakenings. 1 – 3 Acute sleep loss during hospitalization is associated with worse health outcomes, including increased pain, hypertension, hyperglycemia, delirium, and falls, 4 , 5 which can impact clinical recovery, patient satisfaction, and length of stay. 6 Sufficient, high-quality sleep is essential in the inpatient neurology setting, where underlying sleep disorders such as obstructive sleep apnea (OSA) and circadian misalignment are common in patients with stroke, epilepsy, psychogenic nonepileptic attacks, and dementia. 7 – 11 Researchers have examined patient and staff perspectives on potential sleep-disrupting factors in the intensive care unit (ICU), general medicine, and pediatric floor settings. 1 , 12 – 16 These studies have raised awareness of modifiable hospital-related sleep disruptive factors. Although many potential sleep disruptive factors investigated are similar, staff and patients may vary their ranking of top disruptions, highlighting the need to evaluate possible sleep disruptions across different settings and patient populations. 17 , 18 No studies have examined sleep perspectives in the inpatient neurology setting. In addition, most published studies do not include open-ended questions or interviews, limiting the possible causes of reported sleep disruption. Such gaps in understanding specific hospital environments, compounded by the variance in existing literature and discrepancy of staff perspectives, make the effective implementation of sleep-promoting interventions difficult. Our aims for this study were to address the following questions: (1) What perceptions do nurses, providers, and patients hold about sleep disruptions in the inpatient neurology ward? (2) What are the differences between nurses, providers, and patients regarding perceived factors that impact sleep in the inpatient neurology ward? (3) Because inpatients’ sleep may be impacted by different factors, which sleep disruptions most significantly affect self-reported sleep measures? (4) What are the barriers and opportunities suggested by staff in open-ended questions and focus groups related to inpatient sleep? METHODS Study design and participants The study protocol was reviewed by the institutional review board and a waiver of approval was obtained for this survey-based observational study. The study was performed on a 25-bed inpatient neurology unit at an academic medical center. The unit has a central core of workstations, surrounded by shared and private rooms. Shared rooms are arranged with one patient near the doorway and another patient positioned further away from the workstation, closer to the window. Additionally, there is a hallway that extends from the core area to the exit, which accommodates patient rooms. Admissions occur around the clock. Clinical staff were electronically surveyed from October 2020 to November 2020, and focus groups were held in February 2021 with nightshift team members and August 2021 with dayshift team members. Patients were surveyed from April 2021 to October 2022. Eligible clinical staff included physicians, neurology residents, physician assistants, nurse practitioners, and nurses who work primarily on the inpatient neurology unit and provide direct inpatient care. Patients admitted to the unit and who had stayed at least one night were invited to participate. Patients with pre-existing sleep disorders and placed on long-term electroencephalogram (EEG) monitoring were included. Patients were excluded if they were younger than 18, unable to speak English, or transferred from the ICU or had major deficits preventing accurate survey completion (acute illness, delirium, cognitive impairment, or aphasia). Clinical staff were classified by occupation, age, shifts worked, and prior sleep education. Patients were classified by sex, age, race or ethnicity, length of stay, sleep aids prescribed during admission, history of OSA, and continuous positive airway pressure (CPAP) use and tolerance during hospitalization; the collection of protected health information was avoided. Data collection Staff-reported sleep measures Survey development was guided by previously published work and feedback from a multidisciplinary team. 13 The assessment was distributed via REDCap and included a modified Potential Hospital Sleep Disruptions and Noises Questionnaire (PHSDNQ), a nine-item survey that asked staff the extent to which each item disrupted inpatients’ sleep on a 5-point Likert scale, with 1 indicating that the item was not disruptive at all to 5 being extremely disruptive. The nine items include vital signs, tests (such as blood draws and imaging), medications, general noise, bed comfort, temperature, light, pain, and anxiety. Additional survey items included nine quantitative questions about sleep history taking, sleep disruptions, sleep knowledge and training and two open-text questions about barriers and opportunities to improve sleep on the unit. Staff demographics were also collected. Self-reported sleep measures Eligible participants completed screening and survey instruments to better characterize sleep prior to admission and sleep during admission (listed next). Surveys were self-administered unless patients had deficits such as visual impairment or hemiparesis, which resulted in survey administrators reading aloud or transcribing. All survey data and patient demographics from the electronic health record (EHR) were entered and stored on the REDCap database version 11.4.4. The data later extracted for analysis were deidentified with the assignment of a unique participant ID and removal of their medical record number. The survey has been included in the supplemental material. Epworth Sleepiness Scale score: The Epworth Sleepiness Scale (ESS) score is a self-reported questionnaire examining daytime somnolence in eight situations prior to hospitalization, where each item is weighted on a 0 to 3 scale. An overall score ≥ 10 is suggestive of excessive daytime sleepiness. 19 Berlin Questionnaire: The Berlin Questionnaire consists of 10 questions in three categories related to the presence and severity of snoring, the frequency of daytime sleepiness, and the presence of obesity or hypertension to screen persons with high-risk OSA. 20 Consensus Sleep Diary: The Consensus Sleep Diary (CSD) is used to assess self-reported sleep quantity. 21 The CSD assesses self-reported estimates of the following times: closing eyes to fall asleep, sleep-onset latency, or time taken to fall asleep, the number of awakenings after going to sleep, wake after sleep onset, or the total duration of time spent awake after going to sleep, final awakening, and time out of bed. The answers provided were used to calculate total sleep time and sleep efficiency. Karolinska Sleep Diary: The Karolinska Sleep Diary (KSD) uses 5-point Likert-type items to assess patient-reported sleep quality, addressing different aspects of sleep from the night prior. 22 From the validated KSD, we used five of its items: (1) sleep quality, phrased “How did you sleep?”(very poorly [1] to very well [5]); (2) refreshing sleep, phrased “Did you feel refreshed after you arose this morning?” (1 [not at all] to 5 [completely]); (3) restless sleep, phrased “Did you sleep soundly?” (1 [very restless] to 5 [very soundly]); (4) premature final awakening, phrased “Did you sleep throughout the time allotted for sleep?” (1 [woke up much too early] to 5 [slept throughout the night]); and (5) difficulties falling asleep, phrased “How easy was it for you to fall asleep?” (1 [very easy] to 5 [very difficult]). There are additional items typically included in the KSD that address sleep quantity but were not used here due to the use of the more comprehensive CSD. The KSD has been validated against polysomnography, shows good correlations with objective sleep measures, 23 , 24 and has been utilized in inpatient settings. 13 , 25 Potential Hospital Sleep Disruptions and Noises Questionnaire: The PHSDNQ is used to measure how disruptive patients found each item to their previous night’s sleep on a scale of 1 to 5, where 1 indicates not very disruptive and 5 is extremely disruptive. After feedback from our staff survey, we added alarm sounds, staff conversations, neurological (neuro) checks, and toileting to the PHSDNQ. An additional open-text question asking for additional factors not mentioned was included. The survey design and collection methodology utilized in our study are similar to those used in previously published work. 13 , 26 Data analysis We aimed to include as many patient surveys as possible from April 2021 to October 2022 via a convenience sample. When applicable, we grouped evening/rotating/night shift workers and residents/attendings/advanced practice providers for staff analysis to ensure comparable cohort sizes. PHSDNQ responses that were moderately disruptive, quite a bit disruptive, and extremely disruptive (≥ 3) were defined as a significant sleep disruption, serving as the meaningful cut-off to dichotomize the data. The differences in dichotomized PHSDNQ responses across patients and staff were analyzed using proportion tests, and dichotomized responses across patient populations were analyzed using Fisher’s exact test. KSD responses were also dichotomized, using specific cut-offs for each item. Poor sleep quality was defined as a score ≤ 3, nonrefreshing sleep was defined as a score ≤ 3, restless sleep was defined as a score ≤ 3, difficulty sleeping throughout the night was defined as a score ≤ 3, and difficulty falling asleep was defined as a score ≥ 3. Separate logistic regression models were used to assess the association between each dichotomized KSD sleep construct (poor sleep quality, nonrefreshing sleep, restless sleep, difficulty sleeping throughout the night, and difficulty falling asleep) and the 14 sleep disruptive factors. The aim was to identify the disruptive factors that serve as predictors of poor sleep. The results were tabulated, presenting the odds ratio and 95% confidence interval for each model. Multivariate regression was used to assess the relationship between sleep disruptions and sleep efficiency with the stepwise Akaike Information Criterion for variable selection. The optimal model was further examined by controlling for body mass index, sex, race, previous OSA diagnosis, and OSA risk determined by the Berlin Questionnaire. Although multiple sleep parameters were examined, sleep efficiency was used for this analysis because it had the highest correlation with other sleep parameters (R 2 = –0.59, –0.66, 0.68 for sleep latency, wake after sleep onset, and total sleep time, respectively) and is like previously published analyses. 27 Outlier data collected from the CSD were removed from analysis when applicable. Statistical significance was defined as P < .05. R version 4.2.1 was utilized for all statistical analysis. Focus groups were conducted with a diverse group of inpatient neurology staff including four night-shift nurses, five day-shift nurses, three nurse leaders, one patient care technician, and neurology residents. Feedback and open-text survey responses were thoroughly reviewed by the team to identify common themes. The team analyzed the existence and frequencies of concepts (authors A.P. and S.K.) to identify key patterns and trends. We did not follow a formal qualitative review process as the primary goal was to prioritize quality improvement interventions, rather than conduct a comprehensive sociological assessment. RESULTS Study population Of the 115 questionnaires delivered to neurology inpatient staff from October 2020 to November 2020, 49 responses (43%) were received ( Table 1 ). The respondents included a diverse mix of day-shift, evening-shift, rotating-shift, and night-shift workers, as well as nurses, residents, advanced practice providers, and attending physicians. Three-quarters of respondents reported feeling that they received insufficient training in sleep medicine, with more than half never being exposed to sleep education during training. Table 1. Characteristics of staff and patient survey respondents. Staff characteristics Role (n = 49) Clinician 24 (49) Attending physician 4 (8) Resident physician 18 (37) Advanced practice provider 2 (4) Nurse 25 (51) Shift (n = 45) Days 21 (47) Shiftwork 24 (53) Evenings 1 (2) Nights 3 (7) Rotating 20 (44) No. of sleep medicine courses or rotations (n = 45) 0 26 (58) 1–2 16 (36) 3+ 3 (7) Patient characteristics (n = 247) Age (years) Mean ± SD 54.6 ± 19.3 Median (min, max) 55 (18, 95) Sex Female 126 (51) Male 119 (48) Nonbinary 2 (1) Race Black/African American 33 (13) White/Caucasian 202 (82) BMI (mean ± SD) 29.3 ± 7.5 Room type Private 145 (59) Top primary diagnoses Stroke 79 (32) Epilepsy 56 (23) Autoimmune 25 (10) Psychogenic nonepileptic attacks 18 (7) Patient sleep characteristics (n = 247) ESS ≥ 10 99 (40) High OSA risk 101 (41) Sleep aids ordered 100 (41) Preadmission OSA diagnosis 44 (18) Preadmission CPAP tolerance 25 (57) Preadmission CPAP tolerance without in-hospital CPAP order 11 (44) Open in a new tab Data presented as n (%) unless otherwise specified. An Epworth score ≥ 10 indicates excessive daytime sleepiness prior to admission. High OSA risk was defined by score on Berlin Questionnaire ≥ 2. BMI = body mass index, CPAP = continuous positive airway pressure, ESS = Epworth Sleepiness Scale, OSA = obstructive sleep apnea. From April 2021 to October 2022, 247 general neurology inpatients completed sleep questionnaires ( Table 1 ). The top primary diagnoses for patients surveyed were stroke, epilepsy, autoimmune diseases, and psychogenic nonepileptic attacks. Among the patients previously diagnosed with OSA, almost half did not receive CPAP therapy during their admission, despite tolerating it before hospitalization. Sleep quantity measures for all patients as reported on the CSD are summarized in Table 2 . Table 2. Self-reported sleep/wake parameters on neurology inpatients (n = 247). Parameter All Patients Sleep-onset 22:26 (1 h 53 min) Sleep-onset latency, min 40.6 (69.1) Wake after sleep onset, min 60.2 (70.2) Final wake time 06:49 (1 h 44 min) Total time in bed, min 501.0 (136.9) Total sleep time, min 403.8 (159.9) Sleep efficiency, % 78.7 (20.7) Number of awakenings 2.6 (2.9) Open in a new tab Data presented as mean (standard deviation) unless otherwise specified. Self-reported sleep efficiency was calculated as total sleep time/total time in bed. Total sleep time was calculated by total sleep opportunity – sleep onset latency – wake after sleep onset. h = hours, min = minutes, SD = standard deviation. Perceptions of potential sleep disruptions Staff ranked all environmental, patient-related, and hospital-related factors as more disruptive than patients, apart from temperature ( Figure 1 ). Overall, staff ranked noise, testing, and vital signs as the most disruptive to sleep, whereas temperature was deemed the least disruptive. Patients agreed that noise was a top disruptor but, in contrast, ranked bed comfort and anxiety as other top disruptors. Patients ranked more disruptions as “not at all disruptive” compared to staff ( Figure 2 ). Figure 1. Comparing provider, nurse, and patient perspectives on medical, environmental, and patient factors potentially contributing to sleep disruption among neurology inpatients. Open in a new tab Factors rated 1 to 5 (not disruptive at all to extremely disruptive) using the Potential Hospital Sleep Disruptions and Noises Questionnaire. Tests include blood draws and imaging. General noise included all sources. If asked for all three groups, survey responses were included in this figure, as additional factors were added to the patients’ survey after staff input. Figure 2. Comparing staff and patient perspectives on all sleep factors rated from not at all disruptive to extremely disruptive. Open in a new tab *All completed questions per respondent group from the Potential Hospital Sleep Disruptions and Noises Questionnaire. Following data analysis, four main themes regarding identifying and addressing overnight sleep disruptions in the inpatient neurology unit emerged: (1) medical interventions, (2) environmental factors, (3) patient-related factors, and (4) unit workflows. Medical interventions Among the four identified themes, medical interventions exhibited the most significant disparity in beliefs between patients and staff ( Figure 1 ). Ninety-six percent of staff considered vital signs and testing as significant sleep disruptions, whereas only 29% and 26% of patients perceived them similarly. Staff ranked medications (71%) higher than patients (24%). Adding neuro checks to the questionnaire, based on unit staff feedback, resulted in patients ranking this medical intervention lower than anticipated (20%). Across staff, nurses ranked all medical interventions higher than providers, with medications being statistically significant (88% vs 54%, P = .01; see Figure 1 ). Among the various medical interventions, sleep efficiency was significantly lower in patients who endorsed medications as a significant sleep disruption ( P = .03; Table 3 ). Similarly, logistic regression analysis revealed patients were almost five times more likely to report nonrefreshing sleep when medications disrupted sleep ( P = < 0.001; see Table 4 ). However, vital signs, neuro checks, and testing did not considerably impact sleep efficiency. Table 3. Comparing self-reported sleep efficiency among inpatients who did and did not endorse disrupted sleep due to specific in-hospital factors (n = 247). Factor a Sleep Efficiency % When Factor Not Disruptive to Sleep b Sleep Efficiency % When Factor Disruptive to Sleep c Welch Two Sample T-Test Results n Mean (SD) n Mean (SD) t -value df P Medical interventions Vital signs 175 79.2 (1.7) 71 77.2 (2.3) 0.71 138 0.48 Tests 183 79.3 (1.6) 63 76.6 (2.8) 0.85 89 0.40 Medications 188 80.5 (1.5) 58 72.5 (3.2) 2.25 74 0.03 Neurologic checks 197 78.4 (1.6) 48 79.7 (2.4) −0.48 81 0.64 Environmental factors Staff conversations 208 79.0 (1.5) 38 76.8 (3.3) 0.60 47 0.55 Alarms/ equipment 183 79.8 (1.5) 62 75.0 (3.1) 1.40 83 0.17 General noise 165 78.8 (1.8) 79 78.2 (2.2) 0.19 152 0.85 Bed comfort 156 79.5 (1.7) 89 76.9 (2.5) 0.86 144 0.39 Temperature 176 80.4 (1.6) 69 73.9 (2.7) 2.09 107 0.04 Light 200 79.4 (1.5) 45 74.8 (3.7) 1.16 51 0.25 Patient-related factors Pain 171 81.6 (1.4) 75 71.3 (3.3) 2.90 86 0.005 Anxiety 155 81.9 (1.6) 91 72.8 (2.5) 3.05 141 0.003 Stress 164 82.2 (1.5) 81 70.9 (2.9) 3.50 105 0.001 Toileting 181 79.2 (1.6) 65 76.8 (2.9) 0.72 88 0.47 Open in a new tab The mean self-reported sleep efficiency of patients that rated specific sleep disruptive factors disruptive was compared to the mean sleep efficiency of patients that rated the factors not disruptive. Sleep efficiency was calculated by self-reported total sleep time divided by time in bed. Mean (standard deviation) presented as percent. Bolded values indicate significance ( P value < .05). Tests include blood draws and imaging. df = degrees of freedom, SD = standard deviation. a Patients rated each factor from 1 to 5 (not disruptive to extremely disruptive to sleep). Sleep disruption was categorized as a score ≥ 3. b Self-reported sleep efficiency of patients who rated factor < 3. c Self-reported sleep efficiency of patients who rated factor ≥ 3. Table 4. Odds of poor patient-reported sleep quality associated with medical, environmental, and patient factors across five dimensions of sleep (a–e) among neurology inpatients (n = 247). Factor a (a) How did you sleep? b (b) Did you feel refreshed after you arose this morning? c (c) Did you sleep soundly? d (d) Did you sleep throughout the time allotted for sleep? e (e) How easy was it for you to fall asleep? f Medical interventions Vital signs 1.47 (0.84–2.59) 1.85 (1.00–3.53) 1.76 (1.01–3.12) 1.40 (0.80–2.50) 1.35 (0.77–2.36) Tests 1.19 (0.67–2.14) 1.82 (0.96–3.59) 1.55 (0.87–2.80) 1.52 (0.85–2.80) 0.96 (0.53–1.72) Medications 1.55 (0.85–2.87) 4.77 (2.18–12.03) 2.03 (1.11–3.77) 1.55 (0.84–2.90) 1.23 (0.68–2.23) Neuro checks 1.32 (0.69–2.55) 2.43 (1.16–5.62) 1.39 (0.74–2.67) 1.22 (0.64–2.38) 0.79 (0.41–1.51) Environmental factors Staff conversation 1.80 (0.89–3.80) 1.46 (0.69–3.32) 3.24 (1.54–7.32) 1.32 (0.65–2.75) 1.83 (0.91–3.72) Alarms /equipment 1.78 (0.99–3.27) 2.54 (1.30–5.31) 1.94 (1.08–3.54) 1.60 (0.88–2.96) 1.93 (1.08–3.47) General noise 1.63 (0.95–2.84) 1.92 (1.06–3.58) 2.67 (1.53–4.73) 1.53 (0.88–2.69) 2.05 (1.19–3.55) Bed comfort 2.21 (1.29–3.83) 1.87 (1.06–3.39) 2.34 (1.37–4.03) 2.32 (1.34–4.10) 1.83 (1.08–3.12) Temperature 2.07 (1.17–3.75) 3.09 (1.59–6.43) 2.49 (1.40–4.51) 2.21 (1.23–4.09) 1.85 (1.05–3.26) Light 2.34 (1.18–4.87) 2.19 (1.03–5.08) 2.14 (1.09–4.32) 1.52 (0.77–3.07) 2.00 (1.04–3.91) Patient-related factors Pain 1.98 (1.13–3.50) 1.54 (0.85–2.85) 1.60 (0.92–2.78) 1.68 (0.96–3.00) 1.42 (0.82–2.47) Anxiety 2.05 (1.21–3.52) 2.55 (1.42–4.74) 2.02 (1.20–3.45) 2.19 (1.28–3.82) 3.12 (1.83–5.39) Stress 5.31(2.92–10.11) 3.80 (2.00–7.69) 6.36 (3.48–12.13) 3.85 (2.13–7.22) 4.87 (2.77–8.77) Toileting 1.29 (0.73–2.30) 1.73 (0.93–3.36) 1.30 (0.74–2.31) 1.05 (0.59–1.88) 1.57 (0.89–2.78) Open in a new tab Analysis performed via logistic regression with all 14 sleep disruptive factors entered into each of the five sleep dimension models. Disruptions and sleep dimensions were rated on 5-point Likert scales. Data presented as odds ratio (95% confidence interval); bolded values indicate significance ( P value < .05). Tests include blood draws and imaging. a Rated 1 to 5 (not disruptive to extremely disruptive). Sleep disruption was categorized as a score ≥ 3. b Rated 1 to 5 (very poorly to very well). Poor sleep quality was defined as a score ≤ 3. c Rated 1 to 5 (not at all to completely). Nonrefreshing sleep was defined as a score ≤ 3. d Rated 1 to 5 (very restless to very soundly). Restless sleep was defined as a score ≤ 3. e Rated 1 to 5 (woke up much too early to slept through the night). Difficulty sleeping throughout the night was defined as a score ≤ 3. f Rated 1 to 5 (very easy to very difficult). Difficulty falling asleep was defined as a score ≥ 3. Patients with an acute stroke diagnosis were more likely to categorize all medical interventions, including vital signs (39% vs 24%, P = .02), tests (34% vs 22%, P = .04), medications (33% vs 19%, P = .02), and neuro checks (29% vs 15%, P = .01), as significant sleep disruptions compared to the remaining unit population. However, no significant differences were found when comparing sleep log parameters between stroke and nonstroke patients. In open survey comments from staff, prioritizing order completion over inpatient sleep was highlighted as a barrier. Seventy percent of nurses reported waking patients up for noncritical lab draws or medication administration sometimes, usually, or always. Additional barriers included differing time-sensitive order schedules in the EHR, limiting the bundling of medical interventions. For example, one nurse stated, “The nurses try to cluster care together, so if the medication is due at midnight, the nurse will perform the neuro check at midnight and administer the medication, waking the patient up. Sometimes the medication, especially subcutaneous heparin, will be scheduled at off times, such as 2:00 am , and the patient will be woken up for that.” Focus groups with night-shift nurses and patient care technicians also emphasized problematic around-the-clock orders for patients documented as medically ready for discharge in progress notes. One patient care technician mentioned, “There is a large issue that patients are documented as medically ready or awaiting placement but still on Q4 vitals. I am preparing to do midnight vitals and will have to wake up 16 out of our 22 patients soon.” The inability to prioritize order adjustments was noted by one third-year resident, who stated, “Residents have too much going on. Cleaning up orders is not a top priority.” When we asked staff for suggestions to improve inpatient sleep, orders emerged as a central theme. Several participants recommended reviewing all overnight orders and modifying or discontinuing around-the-clock orders during rounds. One nurse expressed, “It is important for providers to go through and review orders for patients who can be made do not disturb overnight,” while another nurse suggested, “Adjusting the frequency of vitals and neuro checks should be encouraged as a more formalized part of the care for all our patients.” In contrast, patients expected overnight medical interventions as part of being hospitalized. One patient said, “I can’t penalize them for checking vital signs. I expect being checked on while in the hospital.” Environmental factors Patients ranked bed comfort as the most disruptive of all potential sleep disruptions on the PHSDNQ. Patients (32%) and staff (94%) agreed that noise was one of the leading sleep disruptions. Temperature and light were ranked the lowest out of all potential sleep disruptions by staff. While staff ranked temperature the lowest, temperature was the only environmental factor associated with significantly lower patient-reported sleep efficiency ( Table 3 ). Logistic regression analysis demonstrated that environmental factors were also associated with higher odds of poor sleep quality, restless sleep, difficulty falling asleep, difficulty staying asleep, and nonrestorative sleep ( Table 4 ). Patients’ open-text comments highlighted additional environmental factors, including cell phone ringers, TVs, or lights on, and disruptions caused by their roommates, including snoring, cell phone ringer, TV, or bed alarm. While staff overwhelmingly suggested modifying orders, only one staff member suggested modifying the environment to improve sleep by measuring noise levels. Patients mentioned using earplugs and eye masks as aids for sleeping, but only 53% of staff members were aware of the availability of quiet kits containing eye masks and earplugs for patient use on the unit. Patient-related factors Patient-related factors, including pain, anxiety, and toileting, were assessed using the PHSDNQ, while stress was measured using the KSD. The logistic regression analysis revealed significant associations between patient-related factors and all sleep dimensions, including poorer sleep quality, more restless sleep, difficulty falling and staying asleep, and less restorative sleep ( Table 4 ). Patients with disrupted sleep from stress were five times more likely to have poor sleep quality, four times more likely to have nonrefreshing sleep, six times more likely to have restless sleep, four times more likely to have difficulty sleeping through the night, and five times more likely to have trouble falling asleep. Additionally, pain, anxiety, and stress were all associated with lower self-reported sleep efficiency ( Table 3 ). We identified additional patient-related factors while examining the open-text comments in our survey ( Figure 3 ), including stress and anxiety related to an unclear discharge plan or prognosis, nightmares, itching, sweating, and acute illness ( Figure 3 ). Figure 3. Word cloud of additional sleep disruptive factors identified in patient survey open comments under “other.”. Open in a new tab The more frequent a particular word appeared within the open-text comments, the larger and bolder it appears in this word cloud. Hospital workflows The following subcategories delineate workflow discrepancies that both patient and staff identified as factors interfering with sleep on the unit. Hospital policies: No established policies empower staff to turn off televisions when disruptive to other patients, as noted by one patient and one staff member. Medication administration policies were also mentioned as potential barriers to sleep. One nurse said, “Nursing can change the times for the medications manually if they are written as twice or three times a day. If the orders are written as every 8 hours or every 12 hours, we are limited. We can slowly adjust those set times medication a little bit each time we give them, but it obviously takes a bit longer.” Room assignments: Patients expressed in open-text comments that overnight room changes disrupted their sleep. One patient even reported feeling disoriented after a room change, leading to a phone call to his wife at 2 am . Staff also recommended assigning private rooms to patients who are more likely to disrupt the sleep of their roommates. CPAP: CPAP mask fit and obtaining properly fitted masks from the hospital were mentioned by several patients in open-text comments. CPAP was not mentioned at all by staff. Multivariate regression analysis: factors influencing self-reported sleep efficiency To assess the relationship between various disruptions and self-reported sleep efficiency, we utilized a multivariate regression model with stepwise Akaike Information Criterion variable selection. The optimized model, which had the lowest Akaike Information Criterion value, included three factors: pain, temperature, and stress. The results of the multivariate regression analysis indicated that patients experiencing higher levels of sleep disruption from pain, temperature discomfort, and stress had a significantly lower sleep efficiency (–7.26%, P = .02; –6.01%, P = .06; –10.05%, P = .001, respectively). However, after adjusting for body mass index, age, sex, race, previously diagnosed OSA, and Berlin risk, the association between temperature discomfort and sleep efficiency became nonsignificant ( P = .24). DISCUSSION Summary of key findings To our knowledge, this is the first study investigating and comparing the perspectives of both inpatients and staff in the inpatient neurology setting using surveys, open-ended questions, and focus groups. The findings underscore significant differences in how patients and staff perceive sleep disruptions. Our study demonstrates that patient-reported sleep efficiency was predominantly influenced by patient-related factors, including pain, stress, and anxiety. Sleep quality was mostly influenced by both patient-related and environmental factors, with stress exerting the most substantial impact. Staff focused primarily on medical interventions as the highest-offending disruptors in open-ended questions and focus groups, while patients did not find them as disruptive as expected. Medication administration was the only medical intervention that significantly impacted patient-reported sleep efficiency. Staff tended to underestimate patient-related factors, akin to previous research showing that nurses tend to overestimate their own role in causing sleep disruptions while underestimating patient-related factors. 28 While stress was included only on patient questionnaires, it was not mentioned in staff survey open comments or focus groups. By shedding light on these nuanced perspectives along with self-reported sleep quantity, our study expands the current understanding of sleep within the inpatient setting and provides valuable insights for improving inpatient sleep. These findings align with previous research demonstrating differences in sleep perceptions between health care providers and inpatients across various hospital settings, including the ICU, general medicine, and pediatric floor settings. 12 – 14 , 16 , 28 Numerous studies emphasize medical interventions as primary disruptors to inpatient sleep. 1 , 13 , 26 , 29 , 30 Some studies point to early morning awakenings in the hospital being linked to medical interventions such as vital sign checks. 1 , 28 However, associations with medical interventions and sleep efficiency or total sleep time have not consistently been demonstrated. 13 , 15 Our study showed that medical interventions only impacted two of the five dimensions of patient-reported sleep assessed in the KSD. Environmental and patient-related factors played a more significant role in all five dimensions, and every environmental factor increased the likelihood of restless sleep. Bed comfort, anxiety, and stress increased the odds of poor sleep across all five dimensions. Staff commonly suggested clustering, rescheduling, or discontinuing overnight orders to improve sleep. Other studies have prioritized clustering, rescheduling, or discontinuing overnight medical interventions in various inpatient settings to improve sleep. 14 , 29 , 31 – 33 In our study, nurses ranked medications as more disruptive to sleep than ordering providers. Differing EHR profiles between nurses and ordering providers may explain this. Ordering providers cannot access the same electronic worklists and cannot easily view future overnight orders and their associated schedules. 34 At the same time, patients viewed these interventions as inherent and reassuring aspects of their hospitalization, which aligns with one study in the ICU setting and one on the general medicine floor. 14 , 35 This may explain why medical interventions did not impact patient-reported sleep quantity or quality as initially expected. Patients may also lack the awareness that some overnight-care delivery is modifiable or unnecessary, especially later in the admission. Barriers to sleep promotion included the misalignment of patients’ overnight needs with nurse and patient care technician worklists in the EHR, and prioritizing order completion over inpatient sleep. Nurses and patient care technicians have time-sensitive worklists to complete medication administration, tests, and assessments such as vital signs and neuro checks as part of their standard overnight care delivery. However, they may perceive these interventions as noncontributory to patient care or nonessential to patient management, especially if the daytime clinician documents that the patient is medically ready for discharge or already asleep. As the staff in this study point out, workflows prioritizing nocturnal awakenings for around-the-clock testing should be further evaluated. Around-the-clock testing may be prioritized to decrease length of stay. While multiple studies have found increased mortality and care differences during weekend admissions, no significant differences in mortality or care have been found by time of day. 36 These findings also highlight the influence of patient-related factors on self-reported sleep quantity and quality, which staff did not rank as top sleep disruptors. We identified pain, anxiety, and stress as significant factors impacting sleep quality and efficiency in patients. Anxiety affects more than one in four inpatients 37 and various factors, including acute illness, discharge planning, and room changes, triggered anxiety and stress in our study. Thus, poor sleep quantity and quality may be considered a normal response to hospitalization. This impact is not unfounded—one study similarly found that patient sleep quality was significantly affected by illness, worry, and discomfort associated with disease and that nurse perspectives were not reflective of these attitudes. 16 Providing patients transparency of treatment plans and involvement with decision-making during daytime team rounds or ensuring that nightshift nurses understand the plan of care to communicate with patients could decrease worry and improve inpatient sleep. Temperature was associated with decreased patient-reported sleep efficiency. We initially considered temperature an environmental factor, but it is likely also a patient-related factor since we did not differentiate between a warm environment or heat intolerance secondary to an underlying medical condition. We found that 44% of inpatients who tolerated CPAP prior to admission did not have CPAP prescribed, and, for those who did, CPAP mask fit was a commonly cited problem. These findings highlight that patient-related factors may be understated in previous studies investigating sleep disruptions in the inpatient setting. More individualized care plans to assess each patient’s specific sleep needs and preferences should be developed to target patient-related factors, such as pain, anxiety, stress, temperature, and ill-fitting CPAP masks. Increased expertise in mask-fitting and a wider variety of CPAP mask interfaces should be made available in the inpatient setting. Limitations While this study provides valuable insights into the perspectives of both patients and staff on sleep in the inpatient neurology setting, there are several limitations to consider. This was conducted in a single center, which may limit generalizability. We asked staff to rank sleep disruptions for all patients on the unit. However, patients with delirium, cognitive impairment, and acute illness were excluded. Some patients were asleep or too exhausted to participate, which may have led to underestimating sleep disruptions. Patients on long-term EEG were not routinely asked about their participation in a sleep deprivation protocol. Surprisingly, only one patient mentioned this protocol as a sleep disturbance in the open-text survey comments. Moreover, patient-reported total sleep time and sleep efficiency were similar across those on long-term EEG monitoring and those not (400 minutes vs 405 minutes and 81% vs 78%, respectively). The study relied on self-reported measures, and inpatients likely have more difficulty than outpatients when completing sleep logs when there may be less concept of time compared to when at home. Further research, including objective sleep measurement, would better capture the entire unit population. Another limitation pertains to the nature of the surveys—which differed by type and aim between patients and staff. While patient answers focused on their sleep from the night prior, staff answers were generalized to any given night on the unit. In addition, all the questionnaires administered were validated for self-administration, but widespread immobility and movement difficulty among the surveyed population caused many of the questionnaires to be completed verbally with the assistance of our team. Furthermore, regarding data analysis, we excluded eight patients because of negative sleep efficiencies, which may have led to an overestimation of sleep/wake parameters. Lastly, 32% of our surveyed patients had acute stroke and 18% had OSA. Although these patients may introduce confounding variables, it allowed for inclusion of participants with sleep disorders typically encountered in the inpatient neurology setting. Subgroup analyses did not reveal significant differences in sleep log measures between these groups and the remaining unit population but did reveal that medical interventions were more disruptive to patients with acute stroke, highlighting the need for individualized sleep promotion plans. Implications The findings of this study have several implications for improving sleep in the inpatient setting. Hospitals should acknowledge the differing sleep perspectives between inpatients and staff and identify the communication, environmental, and institutional barriers surrounding sleep to achieve more patient-centered care. Multidisciplinary teams comprising patients, their families, ordering providers, nurses, patient care technicians, pharmacists, respiratory therapists, sleep medicine experts, and night-shift staff can effectively address future sleep promotion interventions. In developing targeted interventions, these teams should work collaboratively to consider patient-related factors such as pain, stress, and anxiety. EHR vendors must optimize their systems to cluster overnight interventions more effectively. Hospitals should also provide a wider variety of CPAP mask interfaces and enhance inpatient expertise in mask-fitting. It is essential for admitting teams to carefully assess whether a patient was on CPAP therapy before admission and resume therapy as able. Our study revealed that nearly half of the patients who tolerated CPAP before admission did not have it ordered during their hospital stay. Therefore, ensuring the continuity of CPAP therapy can significantly improve sleep outcomes for patients in the inpatient neurology setting. By implementing these recommendations, hospitals can prioritize patient-centered care, improve sleep quality, and enhance overall patient outcomes in the inpatient setting. We found that patients with an acute stroke diagnosis were more likely to categorize all medical interventions, including vital signs, neuro checks, medications, and testing, as significant sleep disruptions compared to the remaining unit population. Inpatient neurology units may specifically target medical interventions in this population. In conclusion, our study demonstrates differing perspectives between inpatients and staff in the inpatient neurology setting regarding sleep disruptions. The findings emphasize significant differences in how patients and staff perceive these disruptions. Patient-reported sleep efficiency was primarily influenced by patient-related factors such as pain, stress, and anxiety, while sleep quality was influenced by both patient-related and environmental factors, with stress exerting the most substantial impact. In contrast, staff focused on medical interventions as the main disruptors and underestimated the impact of patient-related factors. Medical interventions may be overstated in inpatient sleep research, but we suggest EHR optimization to cluster overnight interventions including medications and nursing assessments more effectively. Individualizing sleep promotion plans, involving patients and family during bedside rounds, and updating the night-shift staff on care plans may improve anxiety around discharge planning. Relaxation and stress reduction techniques should also be considered. DISCLOSURE STATEMENT All authors have seen and approve of this manuscript. All work was performed at the University of Rochester Medical Center in Rochester, NY. The authors report no conflict of interests. ACKNOWLEDGMENTS The authors acknowledge the 5-1600 inpatient team for their engagement and Dr. M. Safwan Badr and Hannah Mesmer for their review. We would like to thank Dr. Oskar Wielgus for his assistance with data collection. This work was supported in part by the Department of Medicine Biostatistical Shared Resource and the University of Rochester School of Medicine and Dentistry. ABBREVIATIONS CPAP continuous positive airway pressure CSD Consensus Sleep Diary EEG electroencephalogram EHR electronic health record ESS Epworth Sleepiness Scale ICU intensive care unit KSD Karolinska Sleep Diary OSA obstructive sleep apnea PHSDNQ Potential Hospital Sleep Disruptions and Noises Questionnaire REFERENCES 1. Wesselius HM , van den Ende ES , Alsma J , et al . Onderzoeks Consortium Acute Geneeskunde, Acute Medicine Research Consortium . Quality and quantity of sleep and factors associated with sleep disturbance in hospitalized patients . JAMA Intern Med. 2018. ; 178 ( 9 ): 1201 – 1208 . [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Venkateshiah SB , Collop NA . Sleep and sleep disorders in the hospital . Chest. 2012. ; 141 ( 5 ): 1337 – 1345 . [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Thomas KP , Salas RE , Gamaldo C , Chik Y , Huffman L , Rasquinha R , Hoesch RE . Sleep rounds: a multidisciplinary approach to optimize sleep quality and satisfaction in hospitalized patients . J Hosp Med. 2012. ; 7 ( 6 ): 508 – 512 . [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. DePietro RH , Knutson KL , Spampinato L , Anderson SL , Meltzer DO , Van Cauter E , Arora VM . Association between inpatient sleep loss and hyperglycemia of hospitalization . Diabetes Care. 2017. ; 40 ( 2 ): 188 – 193 . [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Stewart NH , Arora VM . Sleep in hospitalized older adults . Sleep Med Clin. 2018. ; 13 ( 1 ): 127 – 135 . [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Morse AM , Bender E . Sleep in hospitalized patients . Clocks Sleep. 2019. ; 1 ( 1 ): 151 – 165 . [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 7. Sivathamboo S , Farrand S , Chen Z , et al . Sleep-disordered breathing among patients admitted for inpatient video-EEG monitoring . Neurology. 2019. ; 92 ( 3 ): e194 – e204 . [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 8. Duss SB , Bauer-Gambelli SA , Bernasconi