INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(759,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10275820','What''s the Future of Vascular Neurology?','Gottesman RF, Latour L.','2023','s13311 neurointerventional rehabilitate reanalyzed ecass rebutting tunes atypically respondees alteplase interprets expediting endless negate oversimplify disproportional fakes superficially neuroimages expediting incarnations nitroglycerin extraordinarily neurointerventional neurointerventionalists interventionalists antiplatelets subtleties gottesman gottesman bcv meretoja donnan alteplase ingall fallon asplund hacke kaste bluhmki alteplase berge zoppo cd000213 s1052 meretoja keshtkaran saver quallity rabinstein ackerson e46 e99 gorelick kenton ejr scheiber titans donnan strokologist lindley briard zewude faiz labberton thommessen ronning barra lindley falcao nonneurologist kothari brott broderick falcao nonneurologist federspiel e005609 jovin nejmoa2207576 nejmoa2206317 hoyer likosky leira kaskie froehler liou frytak rehospitalization sacchetti neurohospitalist likosky s11936 schwamm zubizarreta zachrison spokes e005903 audebert schwamm boehme zachrison adeoye e2145824 wintermark nael kokkotis giarmatzis giannakou diagnostics12102392 rmebi clincial levers shuaib arian shuaib ijgm s268093 kummer wintermark nael pitfals benzakoun deslys legrand chavva mazurek harraf hs0202 kostpopoulos haass e13758 rohmann piccininni ebinger grotta yamal nejmoa2103879 saver starkman eckstein nejmoa1408827 uniken venema reinink glyceryl trinitrate asap mattingly rahmani expedites svn asif otite grotta lyden brott interventionists maqsood naveed younus khosa e17740 farkas binkley jagolino gildersleeve jagolino bozorgui ankrom chakrabarti 0b013e318294b1cf scharf prabhakaran collaboratives iimprovements cardiovas outcom rost meschia gottesman e499 e516 nejmoa2111749 s12975 bonkhoff grefkes awab439 finklestein sarfo ulasavets opare ovbiagele','Abstract The field of vascular neurology has made tremendous advances over the last several decades, with major shifts in diagnosis, treatment, prevention, and rehabilitation of patients with stroke. Furthermore, the individuals who are providing the care represent a different cohort than those who were caring for stroke patients 30 years ago, with the increasing need for rapid decision-making for acute interventions and a larger workforce being needed to provide the many complicated aspects of care of stroke patients. Understanding the history of the field is critical before one can speculate about its future directions. In summarizing some of the past massive shifts in the past few decades, this review will discuss future opportunities and future challenges and will introduce the rest of this special issue focusing on vascular neurology in a post-thrombectomy era. Although thrombolysis and thrombectomy remain a major part of ischemic stroke management and care, in the coming years, there will likely be further modifications in how we provide the care, who provides it, how we train those individuals who provide it, where it is provided, and what data inform early management decisions. Supplementary Information The online version contains supplementary material available at 10.1007/s13311-023-01374-4. Keywords: Stroke, Neurointerventional, Cerebrovascular disease, Neuroimaging, Vascular neurology The care of patients with acute ischemic stroke has advanced more in the last 30 years than has patient care in most fields within clinical medicine: it has moved from a disease cared for primarily by internal medicine physicians to one under the care of specialized vascular neurologists; it has shifted from being a disease with minimal treatment and with no urgency in its management to one where “time is brain” and acute evaluation has become a key part of its workflow; it has shifted from evaluation in the hospital to early evaluation not only by telemedicine but in mobile stroke units; and it has shifted from being the 2nd leading cause of death in the USA to the 5th. What do the next 30 years entail? Will this be an era where stroke is eliminated through improved primary and secondary prevention? Where stroke care is primarily delivered in the field or by emergency medicine physicians? Where precision medicine allows ideal selection of treatment protocols for ischemic stroke patients both for acute care as well as secondary prevention? This issue will focus on new frontiers within stroke care (with a primary focus on ischemic stroke), where recent advances have pointed to new hope for the future of stroke and its management. It is unlikely that as a disease stroke will disappear, but the improving ability to prevent, diagnose, treat, and rehabilitate has potential to reach a broader population and minimize the massive disability associated with stroke. A Shift to Urgency in Stroke Care Prior to 1995, when tPA was tested in the landmark NINDS tPA trial [ 1 ] and subsequently approved by the FDA, stroke was treated without any sense of urgency, and often, its evaluation led to no change in management [ 2 ]. Even after the tPA trial demonstrated efficacy in reducing 3-month outcome, when intravenous tPA was given to appropriate patients within 3 h, and showed a safe treatment profile, the emergency medicine community in particular was reluctant to support tPA treatment, and it was not until the tPA trial data were reanalyzed to consider the role of baseline ischemic stroke severity imbalance [ 3 ] that the emergency medicine community truly embraced the trial results and became a key part of the chain of evaluation and treatment in acute ischemic stroke. In 2008, the ECASS-III trial expanded the time window for IV tPA to 4.5 h [ 4 ], confirming data from earlier stroke trials [ 5 ]. The need for immediate evaluation of ischemic stroke patients has been increasingly emphasized as more data have pointed to the concept that “time is brain,” a term coined in a 1993 editorial [ 6 ], with estimates that every minute truly counts. In one estimate, each additional minute of door-to-needle time saved grants 1.8 days of extra healthy life, and every 15-min decrease in treatment delay provides an additional 1 month of disability-free life [ 7 ]; implementations to reduce door-to-needle time are associated with lower in-hospital mortality and less hemorrhagic conversion [ 8 ]. This urgency has been embraced in public health messaging, and the American Heart Association/American Stroke Association guidelines [ 9 ] include an emphasis on urgency: “Treatment should be initiated as quickly as possible within the above listed time frames because time to treatment is strongly associated with outcomes,” as a Class 1, level of evidence A recommendation. To facilitate the rapid delivery of care to ischemic stroke patients, teams of clinicians caring for stroke patients began to assemble, leading ultimately, in the USA, to the formation of Primary Stroke Centers (initiated in 2003) and subsequently, Comprehensive Stroke Centers, with European systems following similar structural changes several years later [ 10 ]. Changes in the Who: Who Is Evaluating and Treating Patients with Stroke? Although historically, internal medicine clinicians had traditionally cared for patients with stroke, the evolution of this model has differed across the globe. In the UK, this model remained despite the shifts in available interventions and speed of evaluation, with stroke-specialized internists caring for most stroke patients; this standard protocol has been particularly important given the shortage of neurologists [ 11 ]. This is in contrast to the USA, where the field of vascular neurology was born in response to the clinical needs, with the onset of a new ABPN-certified Vascular Neurology fellowship in 2003, to maximize training and expertise in cerebrovascular disorders [ 12 ]. The question of who is best suited to manage acute stroke care in particular has been the subject of debate, with one leader in the field having argued that stroke is best managed by neurologists given their detailed knowledge of cerebrovascular disease and pathology [ 13 ], but with another expert rebutting that stroke care requires detailed knowledge about comorbidities and aspects of disease best managed by the internist [ 14 ]. Regardless, there is generally consensus that some expertise in stroke is needed, where neither general internists nor general neurologists are likely adequately trained to meet the urgency of decision-making and management choices needed in stroke patients [ 15 ]. A key aspect in the question of who is best suited to evaluate stroke patients is not only the question of who can manage blood pressure or make decisions about tPA or thrombectomy, or optimize secondary prevention, but who is best trained in the diagnostic evaluation of patients with stroke. Stroke mimics are seen frequently, representing one-third [ 16 ] to nearly half [ 17 ] of stroke alerts in some hospital series, a rate that is predicted to increase in coming years [ 18 ]. Although easy access to neuroimaging fine-tunes a diagnosis, ultimately a careful neurologic exam is critical to identify true stroke patients and to allow better exclusion of mimics [ 19 ]. Although it has been estimated that most diagnoses of stroke made by emergency medicine physicians are accurate [ 20 , 21 ], the same cannot be said for transient ischemic attack [ 22 ], which may represent an important opportunity to optimize secondary stroke prevention. A key additional consideration is that the majority of mimics represent other neurologic disorders [ 16 ], so a neurologic evaluation is critical to not only rule-out stroke but rule-in other neurological conditions. In consideration of who might best be situated to make decisions about an initial diagnosis and consideration for tPA, it is reassuring that even those mimics who are given tPA generally do well, with few complications, and lower hemorrhage rates than their ischemic stroke counterparts [ 23 ]. However, as decision-making involving further advances in stroke is more complex, with considerations of unknown time-of-onset, thrombectomy, and most recently, evidence for a specific benefit for thrombectomy for patients with basilar thrombosis [ 24 , 25 ] which may present more atypically and is more frequently missed in the emergency department [ 26 ], the likelihood of misdiagnoses increases, which can lead to denial of treatment in patients in whom there would be a potential benefit. This further emphasizes the need for a well-trained clinician evaluating the stroke patient, to guide diagnostic considerations as well as decision-making and management. Although there is clear value in specialty-trained stroke clinicians, it is likely that the need for these clinicians will exceed the number of available specialists [ 27 , 28 ]. In one study of 44,099 Medicare beneficiaries with acute ischemic stroke hospitalizations from 1998 through 2000, 60% of patients were seen by a team including both a generalist and a neurologist, 17% of patients were seen by a neurologist only, and 18% were seen by a generalist only [ 29 ]. An updated analysis evaluating Medicare claims from 2009 to 2015 reported that 57% of ischemic stroke patients were seen by a non-vascular neurologist and only 18% by a vascular neurologist [ 30 ]. There has been a marked increase in use of hospitalists in recent years, and it is likely that in the past few years and moving into the future, a smaller proportion of current stroke patients will see a neurologist [ 31 ] and particularly a vascular neurologist. In a survey of hospitalists, only 40% of respondees reported consulting a neurologist for all ischemic stroke patients [ 31 ]. Given this shift to hospitalist medicine, establishing clear pathways for appropriate education about stroke care, outlining circumstances where neurologic consultation is particularly warranted, and developing team-based collaborative care [ 31 ] will be critical to adapt to the evolving landscape. Another model that has been increasingly successful and is likely to increase in use in the coming decades is the use of telemedicine to fill the gaps when specialized stroke clinical evaluation is not possible. The use of telestroke as compared to no availability of telestroke was associated with higher rates of reperfusion and lower mortality [ 32 ] and increased and more timely use of alteplase [ 33 ]. Telemedicine has relevance both for overall acute stroke care but also for decisions about acute interventions when expert opinion which may not be available locally is needed. It has been described that stroke is the “ideal disease” for utilizing telemedicine given the short time window and ease of video access and access to images seen remotely [ 34 ]. One additional consideration, however, is whether telemedicine should solely be managed by stroke neurologists or if other specialists can be trained to fill this role. Despite the increasing use of telemedicine, gaps remain in access to specialized stroke care. In one study of 578 Texas hospitals, only 24% of groups of those individuals who were over an hour away from a primary stroke center had access to a telemedicine site within an hour’s drive [ 35 ]. In contrast, however, more recent estimates from National Emergency Department Inventory data suggest that 96% of the US population has access to either a stroke center (91%) or telestroke-capable emergency department (90%) within 60 min from their home (with geographic variation); these numbers decrease for thrombectomy-capable centers [ 36 ]. Changes in the What: What Data Are Being Used to Make Decisions in Acute Ischemic Stroke Care? As more trials expand the eligible population for acute interventions, whether based on wake-up stroke or specific imaging criteria, the burden of work will continue to increase, and a potential need arises for automated systems that can at a minimum perform initial screening for eligible patients. The increasing use of automated artificial intelligence-based software which interprets perfusion mismatch from CT perfusion data, such as RAPID software and other AI systems such as Viz.ai, allows for more expedited decision-making and rapid screening for potentially eligible patients for interventions [ 37 ]. These approaches have been used in multiple clinical trials and are all but routine in most clinical practice, having been approved by the FDA [ 38 ], with the ultimate goal of expediting treatment and expanding access. Decision-support tools such as e-ASPECTS have also shown promise when compared to a clinician-rated ASPECTS score on head CT [ 39 ], an important consideration in determining appropriateness of acute intervention. There are endless additional applications of machine learning and artificial intelligence-based approaches ranging from prediction of stroke based on risk factors [ 40 ], prediction of brainstem infarction based on CT [ 41 ], and decision tools to help optimize thrombolysis rates [ 42 ]; these expansions parallel increasing use of artificial intelligence throughout health care [ 43 ]. These automated methods, however, while improving speed of access to information about potential large vessel occlusions and resultant perfusion deficits, do not negate the need for an expert to interpret the clinical situation and make decisions about appropriate management. Furthermore, they are not without limitations. Certain technical, patient-level, or scanner-level characteristics may influence their accuracy or their interpretation, and these automated approaches mean additional data is provided that may not add value, and may oversimplify a complex presentation [ 44 , 45 ]. Vagal et al. propose a checklist that should be applied to an automated imaging result—and importantly, which should be considered by the clinician—to reduce the likelihood of erroneous AI imaging results [ 45 ]. In addition to potential of harm, concern has also been raised that the cost of commercial artificial intelligence may be disproportional to the benefit added, with an argument being made for testing these tools in large scale randomized trials [ 44 ]. One of the most concerning developments is the introduction of generative adversarial networks (GANs), commonly used to create “deep fakes,” which synthesize superficially realistic neuroimages of a patient, based on orthogonal contrast mechanisms or other patients [ 46 , 47 ]. However, given the breadth of potential beneficial applications in stroke [ 48 ] along with the exponential growth in the science of machine learning, the proliferation of artificial intelligence tools in medicine will be unavoidable and will need to be embraced by the next generation of vascular neurologists. In the short term, however, judicious and critical application remains wise as it is likely that these tools will continue to have the most value when used to supplement and not replace other clinical data or clinician judgment. The use of AI tools may, however, allow for more flexible models in who is making these decisions, how quickly, and where. One additional consideration is that increasing data does not always lead to better informed decisions. Without a clear understanding of the implications in management of certain findings that can be ascertained through these automated systems, decision-making may become more complicated before it becomes easier, as increasing research may be needed to determine best practice in the event of certain incidental AI-detected findings. Changes in Where Interventions and Treatments Are Being Used Expediting stages of decision-making, and potentially increasing who might make decisions at least about aspects of acute stroke care, has been accompanied by a shift in potential locations where care can be delivered or, at the very least, where the diagnostic process can begin. The stroke unit was designed to allow for specialized care of acute stroke patients once hospitalized. These units were shown to improve outcomes, particularly for patients with large vessel strokes [ 49 ]. The assumption, however, was that acute decisions about stroke were made in the emergency department (or in the stroke unit). Because of the emphasis on reducing the time to potential treatment, for those eligible patients, the mobile stroke unit was born [ 50 ], where an ambulance staffed with stroke-trained physicians and nurses (or, in more recent incarnations, stroke-trained specialists available via telemedicine) could not only provide acute evaluation, including neuroimaging, but even initiate thrombolysis. Although expensive and requiring extensive resources to allow optimal availability, mobile stroke units have been associated with improved post-stroke outcomes compared to individuals evaluated and transported with traditional ambulances [ 51 ], with evidence for less disability at 90 days in individuals managed initially by a mobile stroke unit [ 52 ]. In addition to the use of a mobile stroke unit for early thrombolysis in the unit itself, this model emphasized the importance of prehospital care, and other acute interventions and neuroprotective treatments have been tested in this setting, although several evaluated thus far been unsuccessful or even harmful, including FAST-MAG [ 53 ] and nitroglycerin [ 54 ]. It is likely that this model, of prehospital delivery of early interventions, will continue to be used to test treatments that are most promising when delivered very early after stroke onset. Furthermore, beyond initiation of therapies in the ambulance, the promise of early diagnostic imaging in mobile stroke units can expedite in-hospital treatments such as for thrombectomy [ 55 ], representing an exciting pathway for future improvements in care delivery. A major barrier in stroke care delivery is the ability to deliver care more broadly and to underserved communities, but it is unlikely that mobile stroke units will be able to sufficiently access these regions, given cost and inefficiency limitations, which are particularly problematic in regions with low densities of stroke patients. The use of telemedicine will remain important to allow delivery of care to a range of communities, and particular caution will be needed to avoid increasing disparities further (with high-resource communities having access to mobile stroke units and increasingly improving therapy options, while low-resource communities have neither these stroke units nor the relevant therapy options). The need to improve access to care more globally, however, is not likely to be solved by an increasing emphasis on telemedicine. Access to mechanical thrombectomy, for instance, is extraordinarily low globally, with median access in one study of 75 countries of less than 3% [ 56 ], but even improved access to thrombectomy will only address a relatively small proportion of stroke inequities worldwide. Changes in Training and Specialization There has been a notable shift in upcoming vascular neurology trainees in the USA in that more individuals are interested in co-training in vascular neurology and neurointerventional radiology. While this is useful—it certainly seems like an advantage for future neurointerventionalists to have training in stroke—there is a danger that most trained vascular neurologists will primarily focus on acute interventions and not on diagnosis, secondary prevention, rehabilitation, and other important aspects of stroke care. Although this model (training vascular neurologists as interventionalists) may be optimal given the ability of these trainees to manage the entire spectrum of stroke diagnosis and care (and in fact, to carry out the needs given the increasing numbers of patients eligible for thrombectomy, we may need an increasing number of vascular neurologists with additional interventional training [ 57 ]), it is critical to be sure that the other aspects of stroke care remain with stroke-trained physicians, and a shift to an interventional specialty may take away from these other important aspects of stroke care. Perhaps a two-track stroke fellowship option may need to be introduced (interventional stroke vs stroke), to allow for these distinct career paths, yet also to emphasize the value of each of these training options. It will also be critical to understand how this shift to a more intervention-focused fellowship model might influence the already prevalent disparities in frequency of women and physicians from underrepresented groups within the field of vascular neurology [ 58 , 59 ]. Another key consideration in the training of vascular neurologists of the future is the increasing need to understand and practice the use of telemedicine [ 60 ]; this will clearly represent a major part of future stroke care, and there is clear evidence that telemedicine consultants improve their care and shorten page-to-needle times, with greater experience with telemedicine consults [ 61 ]. In the USA, it is estimated that by 2025, there will be an estimated 19% shortfall of neurologists [ 62 ], and it is likely that this need for neurologists will be at least as large for vascular neurologists. According to the National Resident Matching Program, in 2022, there were 108 vascular neurology fellowship programs, accounting for only 209 total positions; 15% of these positions were unfilled in 2022 [ 63 ], and the proportion of graduating neurology residents interested in pursuing vascular neurology decreased from 19% in 2014 to 15% in 2017 [ 64 ]. Given the increasing population of patients experiencing stroke and the increasing number of patients eligible for acute interventions, it may be needed to increase the number and size of fellowship programs. Future Directions in Stroke Care: Optimal Care for All As discussed above, the major focus of recent ischemic stroke research has been increasing the number of individuals who are eligible for thrombolysis or thrombectomy, either by expanding the time window or by improving delivery of care. While this will allow for more eligible patients for these interventions, there are still a majority of stroke patients who are unlikely to ever become eligible for these interventions given the size of their strokes (too small or too big), their time to arrival in the emergency department, or because of certain comorbidities. Additionally, there are many individuals who live in communities where there is no nearby primary or comprehensive stroke center, no mobile stroke unit, and where telemedicine systems are not well enough established to allow equity in acute stroke care. Improving public health messaging and education about stroke may allow for fewer individuals to delay an emergency room visit in the event of stroke symptoms, and other system-level improvements may also increase the number of individuals eligible for acute interventions by shortening door-to-needle times or eliminating other barriers to rapid therapy [ 65 ]. However, many patients will remain ineligible for these therapies, and yet improving acute treatment, rehabilitation strategies, and secondary prevention will need to be a continued emphasis of future research in the field of stroke. Furthermore, these system-level issues in stroke care also contribute to disparities in stroke prevention, care, and recovery, both in the USA and worldwide. It will be critical to continue to investigate the nature and extent of these issues in stroke systems of care and their impact on diverse communities, in order to develop interventions that can prevent these disparities and provide broader access to optimal care. Beyond the acute outcomes of stroke patients, further work will also need to evaluate the long-term impact of different types of strokes, regardless of whether the patient was eligible for acute interventions. The Determinants of Incident Stroke Cognitive Outcomes and Vascular Effects on Recovery (DISCOVERY) study [ 66 ], for example, is recruiting individuals with ischemic stroke, hemorrhagic stroke, and subarachnoid hemorrhage, with an emphasis on people from disparities populations, to evaluate the impact of preexisting disease and the stroke itself on cognitive outcomes in the long-term post-stroke. One important advance in the last several decades is likely to shape the future of the field: it has become clear that making management decisions about stroke patients requires careful consideration of the patient’s medical history, stroke subtype, and stroke location—but also perhaps other characteristics of the patient’s health including genetics and subtle neuroimaging features of the stroke and any underlying cerebrovascular disease. In the CHANCE-2 trial, Chinese patients with minor stroke or TIA who were carriers of the CYP2C19 platelet loss-of-function allele were treated with ticagrelor plus aspirin vs clopidogrel plus aspirin, with resultant lower rates of recurrent stroke in those patients treated with ticagrelor [ 67 ], emphasizing the value of determining treatment based on genetic makeup. The consideration of specific imaging features of an acute stroke patient, which might be expanded with additional imaging evaluation [ 68 ], is also a form of precision medicine. The advances in technology which incorporate all of the available data, using machine learning methodology, will likely further inform our precision-based approaches to patient care [ 69 ]. No two patients can be treated exactly the same way, and it’s likely that a precision medicine-based approach may further guide our decisions about best antiplatelets, best antihypertensives, best statins, and perhaps best acute interventions. These considerations may also be relevant for primary prevention of stroke, which should remain a priority in future research. Future Directions in Stroke Care: Recovery and Rehabilitation There is an increasing emphasis that rehabilitation is most effective when offered early and using procedures resembling real-life use of the impaired limbs. Furthermore, the field of research in rehabilitation is advancing rapidly, and experts in the field have established a shared framework for research priorities in this area [ 70 ]. Therapeutics under evaluation for stroke recovery range from more traditional therapies to brain stimulation or robotics-based approaches and to pharmacologic interventions such as small molecules, growth factors, or even monoclonal antibodies [ 71 ]. Additional promise has been found with the use of telerehabilitation, which has been found to have equal or even better effects on recovery than conventional face-to-face therapy [ 72 ]. The Next 30 Years It is difficult to imagine all the ways that stroke care will evolve over the next 30 years. It is likely that care will be delivered routinely in the pre-hospital setting, with likely additional agents that may reach broader populations of eligible patients. Decisions about acute interventions may be automated up to a certain point, although it is unlikely that doctors—and in particular, vascular neurologists—will be fully replaced in making decisions about patient care and other aspects of stroke care and rehabilitation. Risk factors for stroke will still exist, and stroke will still exist as a major cause of disability. A precision-based approach of most optimal medications for prevention may develop, which might incorporate genetics, metabolism, or other factors that guide the best management for a given individual; this may even expand to acute interventions as well, where one therapy may be less effective in individuals with certain imaging characteristics, metabolic features, or genetic makeup. Major advances in the field are likely, but continued multidisciplinary teams will be needed to apply and interpret these advances and to implement any new technologies and treatments to assure equitable access to broad communities of patients with stroke. Beyond the expected advances and modifications in how care is delivered, we need to be sure that vascular neurology remains an exciting field for future trainees. Furthermore, not only does there need to be continued interest in training clinicians to understand subtleties of acute stroke presentation and diagnosis, clot retrieval, secondary prevention, and directing patients to appropriate early rehabilitation, but the top future investigators (physician-scientists and scientists) need to remain excited about the areas of potential discovery in the science of stroke and cerebrovascular disease. This collection of articles focuses on important topics which have changed or are changing as we consider stroke care in the coming decades, ranging from issues in secondary prevention of stroke to disparities in access to stroke interventions and to reperfusion injury and outcome in minor stroke. Thrombectomy clearly advanced the field, but there are further considerations in the optimization of stroke care and improving its delivery to a wide range of patients and clear priorities for future research in the field of stroke. This issue of Neurotherapeutics maps out some of the exciting future areas which we anticipate will continue to engage the next generation of clinicians and researchers. Supplementary Information Below is the link to the electronic supplementary material. Supplementary file1 (PDF 198 KB) (197.6KB, pdf) Funding Drs. Gottesman and Latour are supported by the NINDS Intramural Research Program. Required Author Forms Disclosure forms provided by the authors are available with the online version of this article. Disclosures Neither Dr. Gottesman nor Dr. Latour has any disclosures. Footnotes Publisher''s Note Springer Nature remains neutral with regard to jurisdictional claims in published maps and institutional affiliations. References 1. The NINDS rt-PA Study Group. Tissue plasminogen activator for acute ischemic stroke. New Eng J Med. 1995;333:1581–8. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ]