):238–244. doi: 10.4274/jcrpe.1594. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 23. Jaeschke R, Singer J, Guyatt GH. Measurement of health status. Ascertaining the minimal clinically important difference. Control Clin Trials. 1989;10(4):407–415. doi: 10.1016/0197-2456(89)90005-6. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 24. Le CY, Truong LK, Holt CJ, Filbay SR, Dennett L, Johnson JA, et al. Searching for the Holy Grail: A Systematic Review of Health-Related Quality of Life Measures for Active Youth. J Orthop Sports Phys Ther. 2021;51(10):478–491. doi: 10.2519/jospt.2021.10412. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 25. Khanna P, Agarwal N, Khanna D, Hays RD, Chang L, Bolus R, et al. Development of an online library of patient-reported outcome measures in gastroenterology: the GI-PRO database. Am J Gastroenterol. 2014;109(2):234–248. doi: 10.1038/ajg.2013.401. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 26. Roe D, Mazor Y, Gelkopf M. Patient-reported outcome measurements (PROMs) and provider assessment in mental health: a systematic review of the context of implementation. Int J Qual Health Care. 2021;33(1):mzz084. doi: 10.1093/intqhc/mzz084. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 27. Churruca K, Pomare C, Ellis LA, Long JC, Henderson SB, Murphy LED, et al. Patient-reported outcome measures (PROMs): A review of generic and condition-specific measures and a discussion of trends and issues. Health Expect. 2021;24(4):1015–1024. doi: 10.1111/hex.13254. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 28. Wong K, Piraquive J, Troiano CA, Sulibhavi A, Grundfast KM, Levi JR. Are validated patient-reported outcomes used on children in pediatric otolaryngology? A systematic review. Int J Pediatr Otorhinolaryngol. 2018;105:63–71. doi: 10.1016/j.ijporl.2017.12.009. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 29. Sadeghi S, Fayed N, Ronen GM. Patient-reported outcome measures in pediatric epilepsy: A content analysis using World Health Organization definitions. Epilepsia. 2014;55(9):1431–1437. doi: 10.1111/epi.12740. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 30. Gabes M, Tischer C, Apfelbacher C, quality of life working group of the Harmonising Outcome Measures for Eczema (HOME) initiative Measurement properties of quality-of-life outcome measures for children and adults with eczema: An updated systematic review. Pediatr Allergy Immunol Off Publ Eur Soc Pediatr Allergy Immunol. 2020;31(1):66–77. doi: 10.1111/pai.13120. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 31. de Wit M, Delemarre-van de Waal HA, Pouwer F, Gemke RJBJ, Snoek FJ. Monitoring health related quality of life in adolescents with diabetes: a review of measures. Arch Dis Child. 2007;92(5):434. doi: 10.1136/adc.2006.102236. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Associated Data This section collects any data citations, data availability statements, or supplementary materials included in this article. Supplementary Materials Additional file 1. Search terms. (14.7KB, docx) Data Availability Statement All data generated or analysed during this study are included in this published article [and its supplementary information files].',605,'2025-10-09 07:10:38','2025-10-09 07:10:38');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(783,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7468001','Gender inequality in leadership and academic rank in academic reproductive endocrinology programs.','Selter JH, Spurlin EE, Brady PC.','2020','abog abog outnumber abog pronoun abog srei outnumber srei programing bb3a7629943642ada47058d0bdcd1521 jolliff leadley coakley sloane lautenberger dandar raezer sloane hofler hacker ricciotti baecher 0b013e318275679d pololi civian dottolo krupat schulkin holzman svider eloy raker stuckey guancial worobey henault tarbell hylek nejmsa053910 enders lindor mcclees lindor 0b013e3181ff9ff2 allshouse skaznik wikiel dukes kelch schaller nejm199610243351706 barnhart puscheck segars scaramucci szalacha palepu moskowitz whi girod fassiotto grewal sriram nosek valantine terrin breeze urech byington','Abstract Purpose To identify gender differences in leadership and academic rank within academic reproductive endocrinology (REI) programs with fellowships in the USA. Methods Official institutional websites of the 2017–2018 American Board of Obstetrics and Gynecology (ABOG)-accredited reproductive endocrinology fellowship programs were reviewed, and gender representation at each leadership position and academic rank (Division and Fellowship Director and Full, Associate, and Assistant Professor) was recorded. Univariate comparisons were performed using Chi-square tests, with significance at p < 0.05. Results Among 49 ABOG-accredited reproductive endocrinology programs, 263 faculty were identified, 129 (49.0%) male and 134 (51.0%) female. Division directors were 69.3% male and 30.7% female ( p = 0.006). Similarly, fellowship directors were 65.3% male and 34.6% female ( p = 0.03). Full professors ( n = 101) were more frequently male (70.3% vs. 29.7%, p < 0.001). There was no difference in gender among associate professors ( n = 60, 51.7% male vs. 48.3% female, p = 0.79), while significantly more assistant professors were female than male ( n = 102, 73.6% vs. 26.4%, p < 0.001). Conclusion While a majority of residents in obstetrics and gynecology and half of reproductive endocrinology academic faculty are female, women are still underrepresented among leadership positions and full professors in academic reproductive endocrinology programs with fellowship programs. Keywords: Women in medicine, Academic medicine, Professional development, Gender disparities, Academic leadership, Reproductive endocrinology and infertility Introduction In medicine, women comprise over half of all medical students [ 1 ]. Specifically, in the field of obstetrics and gynecology (OB/GYN), according to data provided by the American College of OB/GYN (ACOG), women have comprised more than 50% of OB/GYN residency graduates for over 20 years and now make up more than 80% of all graduating residents [ 2 ]. Among all specialties, OB/GYN has the greatest number of female residents [ 1 ]. Furthermore, the percentage of women serving as full-time faculty in OB/GYN departments has also grown significantly over the last 40 years from less than 10% in 1977 to 57% in 2014 [ 3 ]. Similarly, when examining leadership positions in OB/GYN, the percentage of academic chairs of OB/GYN departments who are female has also significantly risen from 0% in 1980 to now 20% nationally, which is the highest proportion of female department chairs across all medical specialties [ 1 , 4 ]. Despite the increasing number of both female OB/GYN residents and faculty members, there still remains a disparity in leadership roles for women across the OB/GYN field [ 5 ]. According to Association of American Medical Colleges data in 2014, women accounted for 55% of all OB/GYN faculty but only 27% of full professors [ 1 ]. Similar studies in gynecologic oncology have shown significant disparities in leadership and academic rank; men outnumber women at both the associate professor and professor levels, and 78% of division directors are male [ 6 ]. However, no studies to date have evaluated the distribution of gender in a variety of academic roles within the subspecialty of reproductive endocrinology and infertility (REI). Therefore, the objective of this study was to identify gender differences in leadership and academic rank within academic REI programs with fellowships in the USA. Material and methods This was a cross-sectional observational study of the institutional websites of the 49 American Board of Obstetrics and Gynecology (ABOG)-accredited REI fellowship programs as of November 2018. The list of fellowship programs was determined through the Accreditation Council for Graduate Medical Education (ACGME). We examined each departmental website between November 2018 to January 2019 to determine the gender of the individuals in leadership roles including division director and fellowship program director. Associate fellowship directors were excluded from our analysis as they were rarely reported on department websites. We then determined the gender of all faculty members in the division of REI within each department of OB/GYN. We noted each faculty member’s academic title as either full professor, associate professor, or assistant professor. Academic rank was determined by faculty biographies and web searches as necessary. We confirmed gender from physician photographs on department websites and pronoun usage. Inclusion criteria included any academic OB/GYN physician included on the official departmental websites as part of the REI divisions. Private practice physicians affiliated with universities were included only if they were present on academic department websites with academic titles. Exclusion criteria included non-medical doctors such as those with a Doctor of Philosophy (PhD) only. Physicians who are not board-certified in OB/GYN such as reproductive urologists and medical endocrinologists were also excluded. Physicians without the academic titles of assistant, associate, or full professor were excluded including clinical instructors as this designated rank was not present at all programs. Three fellowship programs were excluded—Cleveland Clinic, Thomas Jefferson Reproductive Medicine Associates of New Jersey, and the National Institutes of Health—as there was no mention of academic rank for the REI faculty members on those institutional websites. Thus, 46 of 49 or 94% of academic REI fellowship programs were included. The primary outcome of our study was the percentage of female and male gender in various departmental leadership roles and academic rank. Categorical variables are presented as the frequency and proportion. Univariate comparisons were performed using Chi-square tests. All tests were two sided, and p values < 0.05 were considered statistically significant. All analyses were performed with SAS 9.4 (SAS Institute, Cary, NC). Results There were a total of 49 ABOG-accredited REI programs included in analysis of departmental leadership including division and fellowship directors. Forty-six programs were examined to determine academic rank. A total of 263 faculty members’ academic ranks were identified. The average number of faculty members per program was 5.4 (range 2–13). In total, there were 129 (49%) male and 134 (51%) female faculty members (Table 1 ). Table 1. Percentage of females and males comprising leadership roles and academic rank in academic REI departments Female Male p value Total faculty 51.0% (134) 49.0% (129) 0.66 Leadership roles Division director 30.6% (15) 69.4% (34) 0.015 Fellowship director 34.7% (17) 65.3% (32) 0.03 Academic rank Full professor 29.7% (30) 70.3% (71) < 0.001 Associate professor 48.3% (29) 51.7% (31) 0.79 Assistant professor 73.6% (75) 26.4% (27) < 0.001 Open in a new tab When examining division directors, 69.4% ( n = 34) were male and 30.6% ( n = 15) were female ( p = 0.006). Among fellowship directors, 65.3% ( n = 32) were male and 34.7% ( n = 17) female ( p = 0.03). Similarly, full professors ( n = 101) were more likely to be male than female: 71 (70.3%) vs. 30 (29.7%), respectively ( p < 0.001). There was no difference in gender representation among associate professors ( n = 60, 31 (51.7%) male vs. 29 (48.3%) female, p = 0.79). When examining assistant professors ( n = 102), there were significantly more female than male assistant professors (75 (73.6%) vs. 27 (26.4%), respectively, p < 0.001) (Table 1 ). Discussion These findings demonstrate that throughout the USA, women are underrepresented in leadership roles in academic REI programs despite accounting for an equal proportion of faculty members overall. A significantly lower percentage of full professors are currently female than male. However, gender representation was not significantly different among associate professors, and a significantly higher proportion of assistant professors were female than male. The cause of this gender disparity is not immediately apparent in these results. Potentially, the higher proportion of assistant professors that are female reflects more recent increases in women graduating from OB/GYN residencies. Leaders in academic REI today may represent the higher proportion of male REI graduates of prior years. Another, more troubling possible explanation is that women may be promoted at a lower or slower rate than men, resulting in more women remaining at lower academic rank levels. In other studies of gender disparities in academic medicine, women have reported decreased feelings of inclusion in the academic environment and increased feelings of perceived bias toward women [ 6 ]. A lack of role models and mentors as well as expectations and choices regarding work and family can influence women’s career goals and desires [ 6 , 7 ]. Often times, research productivity is necessary for advancement in academic careers, and studies have shown women in research tracks start out with lower grant funding [ 8 ], with initially slower rates of publication [ 8 , 9 ]. Furthermore, compensation often differs between the sexes, with males in academic medicine often more highly compensated than women, and this disparity increases with seniority [ 10 ]. Our findings are consistent with other studies of the field of OB/GYN that have demonstrated gender disparities in academic leadership, promotion, and salaries [ 1 , 3 – 5 ]. A recent anonymous web-based survey of 215 board-certified or board-eligible Society of Reproductive Endocrinology and Infertility (SREI) members reported that women earn $67,000–85,000 per year less than their male colleagues; a statistically significant salary disparity between the sexes remained even after adjusting for age, years in REI practice, and practice type [ 11 ]. Additionally, the findings indicate women are overrepresented in the lower income distribution as compared with men in the REI field [ 11 ]. Our results indicate that women outnumber men in the assistant professor position; women are equally represented with men in the associate professor position, and men significantly exceed women in the full professor position. A possible conclusion is that given this trend, more women may advance in academic rank over time leading to more equal representation in the future. Unfortunately, on review of the literature, this conclusion is not supported by several studies revealing that women have not advanced in academic rank comparatively to men, despite years of recognition of women’s underrepresentation among advanced academic ranks [ 12 , 13 ]. A survey of over 300 SREI members published in 2016 found that women perform fewer surgical cases and fewer IVF cycles than men [ 14 ]; this and studies among other specialties suggest that lower productivity may be a barrier to promotion [ 13 ]. Child care and other family responsibilities have been cited as a potential obstacle to academic and clinical productivity and career advancement for women [ 15 ]. In 2017, semi structured interviews of faculty and senior leaders at medical schools were conducted to further investigate women’s lack of academic advancement; this research identified missed opportunities within academic medical communities to support gender equality by instituting formalized programing for recruitment, promotion, and retention of women [ 16 ]. Limitations of this study include that the data was abstracted directly from faculty websites, which may not always be up to date or may omit information. For instance, portions of websites included faculty ranked as “instructor,” which is the initial academic appointment (before assistant professor) at some academic centers, so this category was not included in the analysis. While websites may fall out of date and fail to reflect current faculty, department websites represent the public face of universities for prospective trainees and patients, and this project serves as a reminder of the importance of keeping department websites updated to best represent the clinical team. Another limitation of the study is the inability to report physicians’ ages and years in practice, as this information was not available on departmental websites; senior clinicians in practice for longer would be expected to have higher academic rank. Furthermore, the study is limited by the lack of longitudinal data, so it is not possible to evaluate time to promotion or whether academic leadership has changed over time. Further studies are needed to assess how gender representation in the field of REI compares with other OB/GYN sub-specialties and other areas in medicine (including other procedural/surgical fields). Studies evaluating the time to promotion according to sex within academic REI divisions would provide important insight into these current findings. More research is also needed on specific strategies to overcome this gender disparity in academic leadership [ 17 – 19 ]. This study is important in illustrating that despite record numbers of women joining the OB/GYN specialty and an equal number of women and men in academic REI practice, gender disparity in leadership roles persists, potentially influencing forthcoming generations of trainees seeking mentorship and role models. Though the reasons for this gender disparity remain to be elucidated, the lack of more women in leadership roles or with the academic rank of full professor suggests a gender disparity within REI that academic OB/GYN departments should evaluate and work to address as possible. Recognizing this gender disparity can be the first step toward attaining proportionate representation of women in positions of seniority within academic REI divisions. Future efforts should include acknowledging this disparity and prioritizing female leadership, including targeted development of women for leadership roles. Conclusions Women are underrepresented in leadership roles in REI despite women comprising half of the REI work force. Recognizing this disparity is the first step in working to obtain more equal representation in female leadership and tenure in the future. Availability of data and material The data generated and/or reviewed during the above publication are not publicly available but available from the corresponding author by reasonable request. Compliance with ethical standards Conflict of interests The authors declare that they have no conflict of interests. Ethics approval As this was an observational study based on publicly available data, Institutional Review Board approval was not required. Consent to participate As this was an observational study based on publicly available data, consent to participate was not required. Code availability Coding was not used in completion of this project. Footnotes Publisher’s note Springer Nature remains neutral with regard to jurisdictional claims in published maps and institutional affiliations. References 1. ACOG: The Obstetrician–Gynecologist Workforce in the United States: Facts, Figures, and Implications. 2017. Accessed 2019. Available at: https://m.acog.org/~/media/BB3A7629943642ADA47058D0BDCD1521.pdf . 2. Jolliff L, Leadley J, Coakley E, Sloane RA. Women in U.S. Academic Medicine and Science: Statistics and Benchmarking Report 2011-2012. Washington, DC: Association of American Medical Colleges; 2012. [ Google Scholar ] 3. Lautenberger DM, Dandar VM, Raezer CL, Sloane RA. The state of women in academic medicine: the pipeline and pathways to leadership 2013–2014. Washington, DC: Association of American Medical Colleges; 2014. [ Google Scholar ] 4. Hofler L, Hacker MR, Dodge LE, Ricciotti HA. Subspecialty and gender of obstetrics and gynecology faculty in department-based leadership roles. Obstet Gynecol. 2015;125:471–476. doi: 10.1097/AOG.0000000000000628. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Baecher-Lind L. Women in leadership positions within obstetrics and gynecology: does the past explain the present? Obstet Gynecol. 2012;120:1415–1418. doi: 10.1097/AOG.0b013e318275679d. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Pololi LH, Civian JT, Brennan RT, Dottolo AL, Krupat E. Experiencing the culture of academic medicine: gender matters, a National Study. J Gen Intern Med. 2013;28:201–207. doi: 10.1007/s11606-012-2207-1. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 7. Cain JM, Schulkin J, Parisi V, Power ML, Holzman GB, Williams S. Effects of perceptions and mentorship on pursuing a career in academic medicine in obstetrics and gynecology. Acad Med. 2001;76:628–634. doi: 10.1097/00001888-200106000-00015. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 8. Hill EK, Blake RA, Emerson JB, Svider P, Eloy JA, Raker C, Robison K, Stuckey A. Gender differences in scholarly productivity within academic gynecologic oncology departments. Obstet Gynecol. 2015;126:1279–1284. doi: 10.1097/AOG.0000000000001133. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 9. Jagsi R, Guancial EA, Worobey CC, Henault LE, Chang Y, Starr R, Tarbell NJ, Hylek EM. The “gender gap” in authorship of academic medical literature - a 35-year perspective. N Engl J Med. 